## Scope

The Labor Law governs every contract under which a person works for an employer, under the employer's management or supervision, for a wage (Art. 5), including government and public-body workers and charitable-institution workers to the extent stated, training contracts, and part-time workers only as regards occupational safety, work injuries and what the Minister decides. It does **not** apply (Art. 7 as amended by M/44) to family members who are the establishment's only workers, sports players and coaches, domestic workers and the like, agricultural workers and private herdsmen and the like, and non-Saudis entering to perform a specific task for no more than two months; those categories are governed by separate HRSD regulations, breaches of which are nevertheless punished under Art. 229. Casual, seasonal and temporary workers get only the duties/discipline, hours, rest, overtime, holiday, safety and injury provisions (Art. 6). Employment in the public sector is governed by civil-service rules, and social-insurance entitlements (pensions, occupational-hazard branch) by the Social Insurance Law, which this file does not cover. The labor dispute forum is now the Labor Courts (Part 14 of the Law was repealed by M/1 of 1435H); this file records only the limitation period and the standstill rule that survive in Arts. 234-235.

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Art. 2 (definitions: worker, wage, basic wage, actual wage, month, continuous service) | "Worker: any natural person, male or female, working for an employer and under his management or supervision for a wage, even if not under his direct control" (M/134). "Basic wage: all that is given to a worker for his work by virtue of a written or unwritten employment contract regardless of the kind of wage or its method of payment, in addition to periodic increments." "Actual wage: the basic wage plus all other due increments ... including commission or percentage of sales or profits; allowances for effort or risk; cost-of-living or family increments; grants or rewards if stipulated in the contract or work regulations or customarily granted so that workers consider them part of the wage; in-kind privileges, estimated at a maximum of two months' basic wage per year unless the contract or work regulations value them higher." "Wage: actual wage." "Month: thirty days unless otherwise provided in the contract or work regulations." Continuous service is uninterrupted service with the same employer or its legal successor; statutory leave, exam absence, and unpaid absence not exceeding 20 intermittent days a year do not break it | العامل: كل شخص طبيعي - ذكراً أو أنثى - يعمل لمصلحة صاحب عمل وتحت إدارته أو إشرافه مقابل أجر، ولو كان بعيداً عن نظارته. الأجر الأساسي: كل ما يعطى للعامل مقابل عمله ... مضافاً إليه العلاوات الدورية. الأجر الفعلي: الأجر الأساسي مضافاً إليه سائر الزيادات المستحقة الأخرى ... ومن ذلك العمولة ... البدلات ... المنحة أو المكافأة ... الميزات العينية وتقدر بحد أقصى يعادل الأجر الأساسي لمدة شهرين عن كل سنة. الأجر: الأجر الفعلي. الشهر: ثلاثون يوماً. الخدمة المستمرة: خدمة العامل غير المنقطعة مع صاحب العمل نفسه أو خلفه النظامي | Every monetary rule in this file that says "wage" (الأجر) means the **actual wage** (basic plus recurring allowances, commissions and customary bonuses), not the basic wage, unless the article says "basic wage" (Art. 107 overtime does). Housing and transport allowances paid with the wage under Art. 61(5)-(6) are therefore part of the wage for end-of-service award and Art. 77 purposes. A "month" is 30 days for daily-rate conversions. The scraped 2005 body of Art. 2 shows the pre-M/134 worker definition; the row above uses the M/134 pop-up text | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 2 (M/44 additions: assignment, resignation) | "Assignment (الإسناد): the service of providing a worker to work for a person other than the employer through an establishment licensed for such purpose." "Resignation (الاستقالة): the worker's written expression of his desire, without coercion, to terminate a fixed-term employment contract without any conditions or restrictions, and the employer's acceptance thereof" | الإسناد: خدمة توفير عامل للعمل لدى غير صاحب العمل وذلك من خلال منشأة مرخص لها لهذا الغرض. الاستقالة: إفصاح العامل كتابة عن رغبته دون إكراه في إنهاء عقد عمل محدد المدة دون تعليق على قيد أو شرط، وقبول صاحب العمل بها | "Resignation" is now a defined term tied to **fixed-term** contracts and to the employer's acceptance (see Art. 79 bis for the acceptance mechanics). A worker ending an indefinite contract does so by notice under Art. 75(1)(a), which the text does not call "resignation"; whether the Art. 85 resignation fractions still apply to that case is an open question below. Staff-outsourcing ("assignment") is a licensed activity under Art. 30 with the Art. 229 bis fine | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 3 (M/134) | Work is the right of every citizen; non-citizens may work only on the conditions in this Law; citizens are equal in the right to work "without any discrimination on the basis of gender, disability, age, or any other form of discrimination, whether during the performance of work, at the time of employment, or at the time of advertising" | العمل حق للمواطن ... والمواطنون متساوون في حق العمل دون أي تمييز على أساس الجنس أو الإعاقة أو السن أو أي شكل من أشكال التمييز الأخرى، سواءً أثناء أداء العمل أو عند التوظيف أو الإعلان عنه | Job advertisements and hiring criteria must not specify gender, age or disability. Read with Art. 61(4) (employer's duty, all workers) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 5 (M/46) and Art. 6 | The Law applies to: (1) every contract whereby a person works for an employer under his management or supervision for a wage; (2) government and public-body workers including those in pastures or agriculture; (3) charitable-institution workers; (4) training contracts with non-employees; (5) part-time workers as regards safety, occupational health, work injuries and what the Minister decides. Casual, seasonal and temporary workers are covered only by the provisions on duties and discipline, maximum hours, daily and weekly rest, overtime, official holidays, safety and occupational health, and work injuries (Art. 6) | تسري أحكام هذا النظام على: كل عقد عمل يلتزم بمقتضاه أي شخص بالعمل لمصلحة صاحب عمل وتحت إدارته أو إشرافه مقابل أجر ... تسري على العامل العرضي والموسمي والمؤقت الأحكام الخاصة بالواجبات وقواعد التأديب، والحد الأقصى لساعات العمل، وفترات الراحة ... والتشغيل الإضافي، والعطلات الرسمية، وقواعد السلامة ... وإصابات العمل | The subordination test ("under his management or supervision, for a wage") is the classification test; there is no separate statutory contractor test. Temporary work (Art. 2) is capped at 90 days; HRSD Regs Art. 1 provides that a temporary or casual contract that continues beyond 90 days becomes an ordinary contract subject to the whole Law regardless of the visa title `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 7 (M/44) | Excluded from the Law: (a) the employer's family members (spouse, ascendants, descendants) who are the establishment's only workers; (b) players and coaches of sports clubs and federations; (c) domestic workers and the like; (d) agricultural workers, private herdsmen and the like; (e) non-Saudis entering the Kingdom to perform a specific task for no more than two months. The Minister issues regulations for categories (a), (c), (d) and (e); breaches of those regulations are punished under Art. 229. Regulations for (c) and (d) must include labor-market and mobility mechanisms and intermediaries' duties | يستثنى من تطبيق أحكام هذا النظام كل من: أفراد أسرة صاحب العمل ... لاعبو الأندية ... العمالة المنزلية ومن في حكمهم ... عمال الزراعة والرعاة الخاصون ومن في حكمهم ... العاملون غير السعوديين القادمون لأداء مهمة محددة ولمدة لا تزيد على شهرين ... تطبَّق في شأن مخالفات أحكام تلك اللوائح العقوبات المنصوص عليها في المادة (التاسعة والعشرين بعد المائتين) | Seafarers on vessels under 500 tons were removed from the exclusion list by M/44 (they were item (e) in the M/46 text). A short-term visiting specialist (≤2 months) is outside the Law; anyone staying longer needs a work permit and a Labor Law contract. Domestic workers are governed by the separate Domestic Workers Regulation, not supplied for this file `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 8 | Any condition contrary to the Law is void; any release or settlement of the worker's rights arising under the Law during the currency of the contract is void, unless more beneficial to the worker | يبطل كل شرط يخالف أحكام هذا النظام، ويبطل كل إبراء أو مصالحة عن الحقوق الناشئة للعامل بموجب هذا النظام أثناء سريان عقد العمل، ما لم يكن أكثر فائدة للعامل | The Law is a one-way floor: contract and handbook terms may exceed it but not reduce it. A waiver signed while employed (including of end-of-service award or overtime) is void; a settlement signed after the relationship has ended is not caught by this article's wording, though it remains subject to the courts' review `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 9 and Art. 10 | Arabic must be used in data, records, files, employment contracts and other documents required by the Law, and in the employer's instructions to workers; if a foreign language is used alongside Arabic, only the Arabic text is authoritative. All periods and dates in the Law are computed by the Hijri calendar unless the contract or work regulations provide otherwise | اللغة العربية هي الواجبة الاستعمال في البيانات والسجلات والملفات وعقود العمل ... وإذا استعمل صاحب العمل لغة أجنبية إلى جانب اللغة العربية ... فإن النص العربي هو المعتمد دون غيره. تحسب جميع المدد والمواعيد ... بالتقويم الهجري، ما لم ينص في عقد العمل أو لائحة تنظيم العمل على خلاف ذلك | Bilingual contracts and handbooks are fine but the Arabic column governs; an English-only contract or policy is defective. Contracts should state the calendar (Gregorian is commonly chosen) to avoid Hijri-year computations of service and leave `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 11 (M/46) and Art. 11 bis | If the employer assigns all or part of its original work to a natural or legal person, the latter must give its workers all the rights and privileges that the original employer gives its own workers. The Minister may take measures to improve labor-market performance and regulate mobility (Art. 11 bis) | إذا عهد صاحب العمل لأي شخص طبيعي أو معنوي القيام بعمل من أعماله الأصلية، أو جزء منها؛ وجب على الأخير أن يعطي عماله جميع الحقوق والمزايا التي يعطيها صاحب العمل الأصلي لعماله | The 2005 text's joint-and-several liability of principal and contractor, and the joint liability of multiple employers, were **removed** by M/46; the 2005 body in the scraped file and the English body still show them. Do not cite joint liability under Art. 11 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 12-13 (M/46) | The Ministry issues one or more model work regulations (لائحة تنظيم العمل) covering conduct of work, benefits, violations and disciplinary penalties, and sets the rules for approving employers' regulations (Art. 12). Every employer must prepare work regulations for its establishment in line with the Ministry's model (the Minister may grant exceptions); the employer may add terms that do not conflict with the Law, its Regulations and implementing decisions; the employer must publish the regulations and any amendment in a visible place or by any other means ensuring the workers know them (Art. 13) | تضع الوزارة نموذجاً موحداً - أو أكثر - للائحة تنظيم العمل ... يلتزم كل صاحب عمل بإعداد لائحة لتنظيم العمل في منشأته وفق النموذج المعد من الوزارة ... يجوز لصاحب العمل تضمين اللائحة شروطاً وأحكاماً إضافية، بما لا يتعارض مع أحكام هذا النظام ... يجب على صاحب العمل أن يعلن لائحة تنظيم العمل وأي تعديل يطرأ عليها | The 2005 body (10-worker threshold, 60-day deemed approval) is superseded: the duty now applies to every employer. HRSD Regs Art. 3 adopts the model in Annex 1; Regs Art. 4 requires a bespoke regulation to be reviewed and certified by a law office accredited on the Ministry portal before submission, allows establishments with fewer than 50 workers to adopt the unmodified model electronically and receive an electronic certificate, and provides that clauses of a previously approved regulation that conflict with the Law or the model are void `[authority — HRSD]`. A handbook or policy is only enforceable as a disciplinary instrument if it is in the approved regulation (Art. 67) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 18 | On transfer of ownership of the establishment or change of its legal form by merger, division or otherwise, employment contracts remain in force and service is deemed continuous; for rights accrued before the change (wages, end-of-service award deemed due at the transfer date, other rights) the successor and predecessor are jointly liable. For sole proprietorships the parties may agree, with the worker's written consent, that all prior rights pass to the new owner; a worker who does not consent may end his contract and collect his dues from the predecessor | إذا انتقلت ملكية المنشأة لمالك جديد، أو طرأ تغيير على شكلها النظامي ... تبقى عقود العمل نافذة في الحالين، وتعد الخدمة مستمرة ... فيكون الخلف والسلف مسؤولين عنها بالتضامن ... بموافقة العامل الخطية | Automatic transfer: no re-hiring, no new probation, service dates carry over for annual leave and end-of-service award. In an M&A, price the accrued end-of-service liability as at closing; joint liability means the seller stays exposed unless individual written consents are collected (sole proprietorships only by the text). HRSD Regs Art. 14 (item 19) treats a change of owner, name, partners or legal form with the same commercial registration as a "name amendment" rather than a transfer of services for non-Saudi workers `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 19 | Amounts due to the worker or his heirs under the Law are first-rank privileged debts with a privilege over all the employer's assets; on the employer's bankruptcy or liquidation they are registered as privileged debts and the worker is paid an advance equal to one month's wage before any other expense, including judicial and bankruptcy costs | تعد المبالغ المستحقة للعامل أو ورثته بمقتضى هذا النظام ديوناً ممتازة من الدرجة الأولى ... ويدفع للعامل مبلغ معجل يعادل أجر شهر واحد | Unpaid wages and end-of-service award rank ahead of secured and unsecured creditors in an insolvency review. Art. 74(7 bis) (M/44) adds a court-ordered termination in bankruptcy proceedings as a termination event | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 32-34, 36, 38 | Recruitment from abroad requires the Ministry's approval (Art. 32). A non-Saudi may not work, and may not be allowed to work, without a work permit from the Ministry; conditions: lawful entry with authorization to work, professional or academic qualifications the country needs and Saudis do not sufficiently supply (or ordinary-labor categories the country needs), and a contract with an employer under whose responsibility he works; "work" here includes domestic service (Art. 33). No other licence substitutes for the work permit (Art. 34). The Minister lists professions closed to non-Saudis (Art. 36). The worker may not be employed, and may not work, in a profession other than that on the permit until the profession is changed by the statutory procedure (Art. 38) | لا يجوز الاستقدام بقصد العمل إلا بعد موافقة الوزارة. لا يجوز لغير السعودي أن يمارس عملاً، ولا يجوز أن يسمح له بمزاولته، إلا بعد الحصول على رخصة عمل من الوزارة ... على الوزير أن يحدد بقرار منه المهن والأعمال التي يحظر على غير السعودي الاشتغال بها. لا يجوز لصاحب العمل توظيف العامل في مهنة غير المهنة المدونة في رخصة عمله | A hiring review of a non-Saudi must confirm: work permit in the hiring entity's name, profession on the permit matching the job, and that the job is not on the reserved list. HRSD Regs Art. 11 reserves, among others, HR manager, personnel manager, labor affairs manager, personnel clerk, receptionist (general, hotel, patient), cashier, private security guard, government-relations clerk (معقب), customs broker, key cutter, and prohibits assigning those functions to a non-Saudi under any other title `[authority — HRSD]`. Regs Art. 14 bars recruiting anyone under 18 or over 60 (experts and doctors excepted) and ties visas to Nitaqat Saudization bands `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 35 (M/44) | The Ministry may, at its discretion, decline to renew a work permit where the employer breaches the Saudization criteria set by the Ministry or any other conditions in the Regulations; the Regulations must provide measures ensuring the worker is not harmed by non-renewal, including transfer of the worker to another employer without the violating employer's consent | للوزارة -لاعتبارات تقدرها- أن تمتنع عن تجديد رخصة العمل متى خالف صاحب العمل المعايير الخاصة بتوطين الوظائف ... وتبين اللائحة الإجراءات التي تكفل عدم تأثر العامل من عدم تجديد رخصة العمل، بما في ذلك إمكانية نقل خدمة العامل إلى صاحب عمل آخر دون موافقة صاحب العمل المخالف | Saudization non-compliance is now an express ground for losing the permit and the worker. HRSD Regs Art. 14 lists cases where transfer proceeds without the current employer's consent: wages unpaid or late for three consecutive months, employer's absence for three months, employer prolonging litigation, expired permit or iqama, end of the documented contract, and (item 21) after 12 months from entry on 90 days' notice, subject to Art. 77 `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 37 (M/44) | A non-Saudi's employment contract must be written and of fixed term; if the contract does not state a term, its term is one year from the date the worker actually starts; if work continues after that term, the contract is deemed renewed for a similar period | يجب أن يكون عقد عمل غير السعودي مكتوباً ومحدد المدة. وإذا خلا العقد من بيان مدته تعد مدته (سنة) من تاريخ مباشرة العامل الفعلية، وإذا استمر العمل بعد انتهاء هذه المدة، عُدَّ متجدداً لمدة مماثلة | Non-Saudi contracts never become indefinite (Art. 55(1) expressly defers to Art. 37). HRSD Regs Art. 12: the non-Saudi's contract is fixed-term however long it runs or is renewed and cannot convert to indefinite even by agreement `[authority — HRSD]`. Consequences: the Art. 75 notice regime does not apply; termination before expiry without an Art. 80 ground triggers Art. 77(2) (wages for the remainder of the term, minimum two months). The pre-M/44 text tied the default term to the work-permit period; the 2005 body in the scraped file shows that older wording | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 39 (M/44) | Without following the prescribed rules and procedures, an employer may not let its worker work for others or for his own account; a worker may not work for another employer or for his own account; an employer may not employ another employer's worker. The Ministry inspects, records violations, applies the Law's penalties and refers matters within the Ministry of Interior's jurisdiction to it; the Interior Ministry refers violating employers' data to HRSD for penalties | لا يجوز -بغير اتباع القواعد والإجراءات النظامية المقررة- أن يترك صاحب العمل عامله يعمل لدى الغير أو يعمل لحسابه الخاص، ولا يجوز للعامل أن يعمل لدى صاحب عمل آخر أو لحسابه الخاص، ولا يجوز لصاحب العمل توظيف عامل غيره | Secondments of non-Saudis between group companies or to clients require the statutory channel. HRSD Regs Art. 13: a non-Saudi whose work is approved through the Ajeer programme is not in breach `[authority — HRSD]`. Regs Art. 14 (item 20) treats moves between branches or entities of the same establishment as workforce re-registration, not transfer of services `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 40 (para 1 M/44; paras 2-4 unamended) | The employer bears the non-Saudi's recruitment fees, residence-permit (iqama) and work-permit issue and renewal fees, the fines for delay in those where the delay is caused by the employer, profession-change fees, exit and re-entry visa fees, and the worker's return ticket after the relationship ends. The worker bears his return cost if he is unfit for work or wishes to return without a legitimate reason. The employer to whom services are transferred bears the transfer fee. The employer bears repatriation of a deceased worker's body unless GOSI does | يتحمل صاحب العمل رسوم استقدام العامل غير السعودي، ورسوم الإقامة ورخصة العمل وتجديدهما، وما يترتب على تأخير ذلك من غرامات يتسبب بها صاحب العمل، ورسوم تغيير المهنة، والخروج والعودة، وتذكرة عودة العامل إلى موطنه بعد انتهاء العلاقة بين الطرفين | Clauses shifting iqama, work-permit, levy or visa costs to the worker are void under Art. 8. M/44 added only the qualifier that delay fines fall on the employer when the employer caused the delay. Return-ticket obligation arises on any termination, including for cause `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 50-51 (Art. 51 M/44) | An employment contract is a contract between an employer and a worker under which the worker undertakes to work under the employer's management or supervision for a wage (Art. 50). The contract must be written in two copies, one for each party, and must be documented (توثيق) in accordance with the relevant statutory provisions and the Regulations; a contract exists even if unwritten, in which case the worker alone may prove the contract and his rights by all means of proof; either party may at any time demand a written contract; for government workers the appointment decision stands as the contract (Art. 51) | يجب أن يكتب عقد العمل من نسختين، يحتفظ كل من طرفيه بنسخة منه، ويجب توثيقه وفق الأحكام النظامية ذات الصلة وفي ضوء ما تحدده اللائحة، ويعد العقد قائماً ولو كان غير مكتوب، وفي هذه الحالة يجوز للعامل وحده إثبات العقد وحقوقه التي نشأت عنه بجميع طرق الإثبات | M/44 made electronic documentation of contracts a statutory duty; in practice this is done on the Qiwa platform `[model knowledge — verify]`. The absence of a written or documented contract never helps the employer: the worker may prove terms by any evidence, the employer cannot. HRSD Regs Annex 5 contains the model contracts referred to in Art. 52 `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 52 (M/46; para 1 M/44) | Subject to Art. 37, the Ministry issues a model for each type of employment contract, which must contain at minimum: the employer's name and place; the worker's name, nationality, identity proof and residence address; the agreed wage including benefits and allowances; type and place of work; date of joining; term if fixed; and each party's basic rights and obligations. The contract must follow the model; the parties may add clauses that do not conflict with the Law, its Regulations and implementing decisions | تضع الوزارة نموذجاً موحداً لكل نوع من أنواع عقد العمل، يحتوي بصورة أساسية على: اسم صاحب العمل ومكانه، واسم العامل وجنسيته، وما يلزم لإثبات شخصيته، وعنوان إقامته، والأجر المتفق عليه بما في ذلك المزايا والبدلات، ونوع العمل ومكانه، وتاريخ الالتحاق به، ومدة العقد إن كان محدد المدة، وحقوق كل طرف والتزاماته الأساسية | Contract-review checklist: all listed items present, wage broken down into basic wage and allowances (the split drives Art. 107 overtime and Art. 86 end-of-service exclusions), place of work stated (drives Art. 58 relocation), term stated (drives Arts. 55, 75, 77). A bespoke contract that omits a model term is defective rather than void; the Law supplies the missing term (Art. 95 for wage) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 53 (M/44) | If the worker is subject to probation, the contract must expressly provide for it and clearly state its duration, which may not in total and in all cases exceed 180 days; the Regulations state the related provisions, including which leaves are not counted; either party may terminate the contract during probation | إذا كان العامل خاضعاً للتجربة، وجب النص على ذلك صراحة في عقد العمل، وتحديد مدتها بوضوح، على ألا يزيد مجموع المدة في جميع الأحوال على (مائة وثمانين) يوماً ... ولكل من الطرفين الحق في إنهاء العقد خلال هذه المدة | The 90-day default with written extension to 180 (M/46) is gone: parties may agree up to 180 days from the outset. HRSD Regs Art. 19: the probation must be stated expressly; it may be split into multiple periods provided they are consecutive and stated clearly at signing; Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, National Day, Founding Day and sick leave are excluded from the count `[authority — HRSD]`. The M/46 wording allowing the contract to reserve the termination right to one party only has been dropped; under the M/44 text each party has the right. No probation = no Art. 80(6) ground | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 54 (M/46) | A worker may not be placed on probation more than once by the same employer; by exception, the parties may agree in writing on another probation if it is in a different profession or work, or if at least six months have elapsed since the end of the previous relationship. If the contract is terminated during probation, neither party is entitled to compensation and the worker is not entitled to an end-of-service award for that period | لا يجوز وضع العامل تحت التجربة أكثر من مرة واحدة لدى صاحب عمل واحد ... وإذا أنهي العقد خلال فترة التجربة فإن أيًّا من الطرفين لا يستحق تعويضاً، كما لا يستحق العامل مكافأة نهاية الخدمة عن ذلك | Termination during a valid probation costs nothing beyond wages to date and accrued leave (Art. 111). Re-hires within six months into the same role cannot be re-probated. The 2005 body (second probation capped at 90 days) is superseded | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 55 (M/46) | A fixed-term contract ends on expiry; if the parties continue to perform it, it is deemed renewed for an indefinite term, subject to Art. 37 for non-Saudis. If the contract contains a renewal clause for a similar or specified term, it renews for that term; if it is renewed three consecutive times, or the original term plus renewals reaches four years, whichever is less, and the parties continue to perform it, it becomes an indefinite-term contract | ينتهي عقد العمل المحدد المدة بانقضاء مدته، فإذا استمر طرفاه في تنفيذه عُدَّ العقدُ مجدداً لمدة غير محددة ... فإن تعدد التجديد ثلاث مرات متتالية، أو بلغت مدة العقد الأصلي مع مدة التجديد أربع سنوات أيهما أقل واستمر الطرفان في تنفيذه؛ تحوّل العقد إلى عقد غير محدد المدة | For **Saudi** workers: continued work after expiry without a renewal clause converts immediately to indefinite; with an auto-renewal clause, conversion happens after the third consecutive renewal or four years' total term, whichever is earlier, plus continued performance. For **non-Saudis** conversion never happens (Art. 37 and Regs Art. 12). The 2005 body says two renewals / three years; that is superseded. Conversion changes the exposure from Art. 77(2) (remaining term) to Art. 75 notice plus Art. 77(1) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 56-57 | Whenever a contract is renewed for a fixed term, the renewal period is deemed an extension of the original term for computing rights that depend on length of service (Art. 56). A contract for a specific piece of work ends on completion of that work (Art. 57) | تعد المدة التي تجدد إليها العقد امتداداً للمدة الأصلية في تحديد حقوق العامل التي تدخل مدة الخدمة في حسابها. إذا كان العقد من أجل القيام بعمل معين فإنه ينتهي بإنجاز العمل المتفق عليه | Service for end-of-service award and annual-leave uplift runs from the first contract's start date across all renewals; a "new contract" on renewal does not reset service. Do not treat a renewal as an Art. 74(2) termination followed by re-hire | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 58 (M/46), 59, 60 | The employer may not, without the worker's written consent, transfer him from his original place of work to another requiring a change of residence; in exigent circumstances and for no more than 30 days a year the employer may assign the worker elsewhere without consent, bearing travel and accommodation costs (Art. 58). A monthly-paid worker may not be moved to daily, weekly, piece or hourly pay without written consent and without prejudice to acquired rights (Art. 59). Without prejudice to Art. 38, a worker may not be assigned work materially different from the agreed work without his written consent, except in cases of necessity for no more than 30 days a year (Art. 60) | لا يجوز لصاحب العمل أن ينقل العامل بغير موافقته – كتابةً – من مكان عمله الأصلي إلى مكان آخر يقتضي تغيير محل إقامته ... لا يجوز تكليف العامل بعمل يختلف اختلافاً جوهريًّا عن العمل المتفق عليه بغير موافقته الكتابية، إلا في حالات الضرورة ... ولمدة لا تتجاوز ثلاثين يوماً في السنة | Mobility and flexibility clauses work: HRSD Regs Art. 20 provides that a contract clause fixing the place of work as anywhere in the Kingdom, or allowing relocation as work requires, counts as the written consent Art. 58 demands `[authority — HRSD]`. Unilateral demotion, pay-structure change or transfer beyond these limits gives the worker an Art. 81(3) constructive-termination ground | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 61 (paras 4-6 M/44) and Art. 62 | Employer duties: (1) no forced labor, no withholding wages without a judicial instrument, respectful treatment; (2) time off to exercise statutory rights without wage deduction; (3) facilitate officials' tasks; (4) refrain from anything that would nullify or weaken equal opportunity or treatment in employment and occupation, whether by exclusion, distinction or preference among applicants or workers on the basis of race, colour, gender, age, disability, marital status or any other form of discrimination; (5) provide appropriate housing or an appropriate cash allowance paid with the wage; (6) provide appropriate transport from residence to workplace or an appropriate cash allowance paid with the wage. A worker who reports ready for work and is prevented only by a cause attributable to the employer is entitled to wages for that period (Art. 62) | أن يمتنع عن القيام بكل ما من شأنه إبطال أو إضعاف تطبيق تكافؤ الفرص أو المعاملة في الاستخدام والمهنة ... على أساس العرق أو اللون أو الجنس أو السن أو الإعاقة أو الحالة الاجتماعية أو أي شكل من أشكال التمييز الأخرى. أن يوفر السكن اللائق لعماله. وله أن يستعيض عن ذلك ببدل نقدي مناسب يدفعه لهم مع الأجر. أن يوفر وسيلة مواصلات مناسبة لعماله ... وله أن يستعيض عن ذلك ببدل نقدي مناسب | Since 19 February 2025 every worker (Saudi or not) must receive housing and transport or cash allowances; the Law sets no amount ("appropriate"). HRSD Regs Art. 21 applies the housing duty to the private and non-profit sectors and defines forced labor `[authority — HRSD]`. Because the allowances are paid "with the wage" they fall within the actual wage (Art. 2) for end-of-service award and compensation unless a court holds otherwise `[model knowledge — verify]`. Para (4) is the general anti-discrimination duty; there is no statutory damages formula for breach (penalties under Art. 229) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 64 (M/46) and Art. 65 | On expiry of the contract the employer must, on request, give a free service certificate stating the joining date, end date, profession and last wage, and may not include anything that would damage the worker's reputation or job prospects, and must return all certificates and documents deposited by the worker (Art. 64). Worker duties include performing the work per the profession's standards and lawful instructions, care of the employer's equipment, good conduct, assistance in emergencies without extra pay, submitting to medical examinations, and keeping the employer's technical, commercial and industrial secrets (Art. 65) | أن يعطي العامل – بناءً على طلبه – شهادة خدمة دون مقابل ... ولا يجوز لصاحب العمل تضمين الشهادة ما قد يسيء إلى سمعة العامل ... أن يحفظ الأسرار الفنية والتجارية والصناعية للمواد التي ينتجها ... وجميع الأسرار المهنية المتعلقة بالعمل أو المنشأة | Negative references are prohibited in the certificate; breach of the Art. 65(6) confidentiality duty during employment is an Art. 80(9) dismissal ground, and post-termination confidentiality may be extended by an Art. 83(2) clause. Retaining a worker's passport is not an Art. 64 issue but is prohibited by other instruments `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 66-67 | The disciplinary penalties an employer may impose are: warning; fine; withholding or deferring an increment for up to one year where the employer grants increments; deferring promotion for up to one year where the employer grants promotions; suspension without pay; dismissal in the cases provided by the Law. No penalty not provided in the Law or in the work regulations may be imposed | الجزاءات التأديبية التي يجوز لصاحب العمل توقيعها على العامل: الإنذار. الغرامة. الحرمان من العلاوة أو تأجيلها ... تأجيل الترقية ... الإيقاف عن العمل مع الحرمان من الأجر. الفصل من العمل في الحالات المقررة في النظام. لا يجوز لصاحب العمل أن يوقع على العامل جزاءً غير وارد في هذا النظام أو في لائحة تنظيم العمل | Closed list. Demotion, pay cut, transfer as punishment, or forfeiture of bonus are not permitted penalties unless the approved work regulations provide them and they fit within the list. A policy that invents a penalty is unenforceable | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 68-70 | No harsher penalty for repetition if 180 days have passed since the worker was notified of the penalty for the previous violation (Art. 68). A worker may not be charged with a violation discovered more than 30 days earlier, and no penalty may be imposed more than 30 days after the investigation concludes and the violation is established (Art. 69). No penalty for conduct outside the workplace unless connected to the work, the employer or the responsible manager; no fine exceeding five days' wage for one violation; only one penalty per violation; fines deducted may not exceed five days' wage per month; unpaid suspension may not exceed five days per month (Art. 70) | لا يجوز اتهام العامل بمخالفة مضى على كشفها أكثر من ثلاثين يوماً، ولا يجوز توقيع جزاء تأديبي بعد تاريخ انتهاء التحقيق في المخالفة وثبوتها في حق العامل بأكثر من ثلاثين يوماً ... لا يجوز أن يوقع على العامل عن المخالفة الواحدة غرامة تزيد قيمتها على أجرة خمسة أيام، ولا توقيع أكثر من جزاء واحد على المخالفة الواحدة | Two 30-day clocks: discovery-to-charge and finding-to-penalty. An investigation that drags past 30 days from its conclusion loses the right to penalise. For dismissal under Art. 80 the same Art. 69 clocks apply because dismissal is an Art. 66(6) penalty `[model knowledge — verify]`. Fines are capped at five days' wage per violation and per month; suspension five days per month | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 71, 72 (M/44), 73 (M/46) | No disciplinary penalty without first notifying the worker in writing of the charge, interrogating him, hearing his defence and recording this in minutes placed in his file; oral interrogation is allowed for minor violations punishable by warning or a fine not exceeding one day's wage, if minuted (Art. 71). The penalty decision must be notified in writing (registered mail to the file address if he refuses or is absent); the worker may object in writing to the competent department at the employer within 30 days (excluding official holidays) of notification; if the objection is rejected or not decided in writing within 15 days of filing, he may appeal to the Labor Courts within 30 days (excluding official holidays) from the rejection or the lapse of the 15 days, whichever is earlier (Art. 72). Fines must be recorded in a special register and may be spent only for the benefit of the establishment's workers, by the labor committee or, if none, with the Ministry's approval (Art. 73) | لا يجوز توقيع جزاء تأديبي على العامل إلا بعد إبلاغه كتابة بما نسب إليه واستجوابه وتحقيق دفاعه وإثبات ذلك في محضر يودع في ملفه الخاص ... وله التظلم كتابة للجهة المختصة لدى صاحب العمل خلال (ثلاثين) يوماً ... فإن رُفض تظلمه أو لم يُبت فيه كتابة خلال (خمسة عشر) يوماً من تقديمه كان له حق الاعتراض أمام المحاكم العمالية ... خلال (ثلاثين) يوماً | M/44 inserted a mandatory internal grievance step (30 days to object, 15 days for the employer to answer) before the Labor Court appeal. Investigation files must show: written charge, interview, defence, signed minutes. The pre-M/44 route (15 days direct to the dispute body) shown in the scraped body is superseded. Fines cannot be kept by the employer | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 74 (M/46; para 4 M/134; 3 bis and 7 bis M/44) | The contract ends: (1) by agreement, with the worker's written consent; (2) on expiry of the fixed term unless expressly renewed under the Law; (3) at the will of either party in indefinite contracts as provided in Art. 75; (3 bis) by resignation; (4) when the worker reaches retirement age under the Social Insurance Law unless the parties agree to continue; (5) force majeure; (6) permanent closure of the establishment; (7) termination of the activity in which the worker works, unless otherwise agreed; (7 bis) a decision or final judgment of the competent court terminating the contract in bankruptcy proceedings under the Bankruptcy Law; (8) any other case provided by another law | ينتهي عقد العمل في أي من الأحوال الآتية: إذا اتفق الطرفان على إنهائه، بشرط أن تكون موافقة العامل كتابية ... 3 (مكرر)- الاستقالة ... بلوغ العامل سن التقاعد وفق ما تقضي به أحكام نظام التأمينات الاجتماعية ... القوة القاهرة. إغلاق المنشأة نهائياً. إنهاء النشاط الذي يعمل فيه العامل، ما لم يُتفق على غير ذلك. 7 (مكرر)- صدور قرار أو حكم نهائي من المحكمة المختصة؛ بإنهاء عقد العامل في أي من إجراءات الإفلاس | Redundancy is not a listed event; a restructuring dismissal must be framed as termination of the activity (7) with the end-of-service award payable, or as an Art. 75 "legitimate reason" termination (notice plus award, with Art. 77 exposure if the reason is not accepted). The 60/55 retirement ages in the scraped body and in the M/46 text were replaced by reference to the Social Insurance Law (M/134); the current GOSI retirement age is not in the text supplied `[model knowledge — verify]`. Mutual termination requires the worker's written consent | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 75 (M/44) | Indefinite-term contract with a monthly wage: either party may terminate for a legitimate reason; if the worker terminates he must give the employer written notice at least 30 days before the termination date; if the employer terminates it must give the worker written notice at least 60 days before. Indefinite-term contract with a non-monthly wage: the terminating party, worker or employer, must give at least 30 days' written notice | إذا كان العقد غير محدد المدة وكان الأجر يدفع شهريًّا، جاز لأي من طرفيه إنهاؤه بناءً على سبب مشروع، وفق ما يلي: أ- إذا كان الإنهاء من طرف العامل، فيجب عليه أن يوجه إشعاراً كتابيًّا بذلك لصاحب العمل قبل (ثلاثين) يوماً على الأقل ... ب- إذا كان الإنهاء من طرف صاحب العمل، فيجب عليه أن يوجه إشعاراً كتابيًّا بذلك للعامل قبل (ستين) يوماً على الأقل | Asymmetric notice since 19 February 2025: employer 60 days, worker 30 days (monthly-paid). The M/46 text (60 days for both, or a longer contractual period) and the 2005 body (30/15) are superseded. The contract may set longer notice than the statutory minimum but not shorter (Art. 8). Notice must be written and must state the legitimate reason; the Law does not define "legitimate reason" (سبب مشروع) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 76 (M/46) | If the party terminating an indefinite-term contract does not observe the notice period under Art. 75, it must pay the other party, for the notice period, an amount equal to the worker's wage for that period, unless the parties agreed on more | إذا لم يراع الطرف الذي أنهى العقد غير المحدد المدة المهلة المحدد للإشعار وفقاً للمادة (الخامسة والسبعين) من هذا النظام، فإنه يلتزم بأن يدفع للطرف الآخر عن مهلة الإشعار مبلغاً مساوياً لأجر العامل عن المهلة نفسها، ما لم يتفق الطرفان على أكثر من ذلك | Pay in lieu = actual wage (Art. 2) for the full statutory or contractual notice period (60 days for an employer-initiated termination of a monthly-paid worker). The M/46 text dropped the 2005 wording on "the remainder" of the notice period, so part-notice does not reduce the payment on the face of the text; courts may still pro-rate `[model knowledge — verify]`. The worker who leaves without notice owes the employer the same amount, usually set off under Art. 88 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 77 (M/46; not changed by M/44) | Unless the contract provides a specific compensation for termination by either party for an illegitimate reason, the party harmed by the termination is entitled to compensation as follows: (1) indefinite-term contract: fifteen days' wage for each year of the worker's service; (2) fixed-term contract: the wage for the remaining period of the contract; (3) the compensation under (1) and (2) may not be less than the worker's wage for two months | ما لم يتضمن العقد تعويضاً محدداً مقابل إنهائه من أحد الطرفين لسبب غير مشروع، يستحق الطرف المتضرر من إنهاء العقد تعويضاً على النحو الآتي: 1 - أجر خمسة عشر يوماً عن كل سنة من سنوات خدمة العامل، إذا كان العقد غير محدد المدة. 2 - أجر المدة الباقية من العقد إذا كان العقد محدد المدة. 3 - يجب ألا يقل التعويض المشار إليه في الفقرتين (1) و(2) من هذه المادة عن أجر العامل لمدة شهرين | **Computation inputs.** Wage = actual wage (Art. 2), last wage in practice `[model knowledge — verify]`; daily wage = monthly wage / 30 (Art. 2 "month"). Indefinite: 15 × daily wage × years of service (the text says "for each year"; treatment of fractional years is not stated, see open questions). Fixed: monthly wage × months remaining to expiry (plus any partial month pro rata). Floor: 2 × monthly wage in both cases. The contractual-compensation carve-out lets the parties fix a different amount; whether a lower amount survives Art. 8 is open. This compensation is **in addition to** the end-of-service award (Art. 84) and pay in lieu of notice (Art. 76) where those apply. The 2005 body ("compensation assessed by the Commission") in the scraped file and the English body is superseded; M/44 did not touch Art. 77 (confirmed against the portal pop-ups and the M/44 list in the English appendix) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 78 (M/46) | If notice is given by the employer, the worker is entitled, during the notice period, to be absent one full day per week or eight hours during the week, with pay, to look for other work; the worker chooses the day or hours and must inform the employer at least the day before. The employer may release the worker from work during the notice period, with his service counted as continuous until the end of the period and the employer bound by the consequences, in particular the worker's wage for the notice period | إذا كان الإشعار من جانب صاحب العمل، فيحق للعامل أن يتغيب خلال مهلة الإشعار يوماً كاملاً في الأسبوع أو ثماني ساعات أثناء الأسبوع، وذلك للبحث عن عمل آخر مع استحقاقه لأجر هذا اليوم ... ولصاحب العمل أن يعفي العامل من العمل أثناء مهلة الإشعار مع احتساب مدة خدمته مستمرة إلى حين انتهاء تلك المهلة | Garden leave is expressly permitted and counts as service (so accrues end-of-service award and annual leave). The 2005 Art. 78 right to demand **reinstatement** for dismissal without legitimate reason was repealed by M/46 and replaced by this text; the remedy for unfair dismissal is now the Art. 77 formula, not reinstatement. Do not advise that reinstatement is available under Art. 78 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 79 and Art. 79 bis (M/44) | The contract does not end on the employer's death unless his person was material to the contract; it ends on the worker's death or on his incapacity to perform the work certified by an authorised medical certificate (Art. 79). Resignation (Art. 79 bis): (1) a resignation request is deemed accepted if the employer does not respond within 30 days of submission; the employer may defer acceptance for up to 60 days where work requires, by a reasoned written explanation given to the worker before the 30 days lapse, the deferral running from delivery of that explanation; (2) the contract ends on the employer's acceptance, on the lapse of the 30 days without response, or on the lapse of the deferral period; (3) the worker may withdraw the resignation within seven days of submission unless the employer has already accepted it; (4) a resignation may not state a deferred date; (5) the contract remains in force during the resignation period and both parties must perform it; (6) a worker whose contract ends by resignation is entitled to all his statutory rights | يُعد طلب الاستقالة المقدم مقبولاً إذا مضى على تقديمه (ثلاثون) يوماً دون ردٍّ من صاحب العمل. ولصاحب العمل تأجيل قبول طلب الاستقالة مدة لا تزيد على (ستين) يوماً إذا اقتضت مصلحة العمل ذلك، ووفق إيضاح مسبب مكتوب ... للعامل العدول عن طلب الاستقالة خلال مدة لا تتجاوز (سبعة) أيام ... لا يصح أن يُحدد في طلب الاستقالة تاريخ مؤجل لها ... يستحق العامل الذي انتهى عقده بالاستقالة جميع حقوقه المقررة بموجب هذا النظام | Resignation is a statutory mechanism for **fixed-term** contracts (Art. 2 definition): the worker no longer owes Art. 77(2) compensation for leaving a fixed-term contract early if he resigns under 79 bis and the employer accepts or fails to respond. Maximum time the employer can hold a resigning worker: 30 days plus up to 60 days' deferral = 90 days. Item (6) preserves end-of-service award, subject to the Art. 85 fractions (see open questions). Employers should diarise the 30-day response window; silence is acceptance | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 80 (M/46) | The employer may not terminate without award, notice or compensation except in the following cases, and provided the worker is given the opportunity to state his reasons for objecting: (1) assault on the employer, responsible manager, a superior or a subordinate during or because of work; (2) failure to perform essential contractual obligations, disobedience of lawful orders, or deliberate breach of posted safety instructions despite written warning; (3) proven bad conduct or an act against honour or honesty; (4) deliberate act or omission intended to cause the employer material loss, provided the employer reports to the authorities within 24 hours of learning of it; (5) forgery to obtain the job; (6) the worker is on probation; (7) absence without legitimate reason for more than 30 days in one contractual year or more than 15 consecutive days, provided a written warning was given after 20 days' absence in the first case and 10 days in the second; (8) unlawful exploitation of position for personal gain; (9) disclosure of industrial or commercial secrets of the work | لا يجوز لصاحب العمل فسخ العقد دون مكافأة العامل، أو إشعاره، أو تعويضه؛ إلا في الحالات الآتية، وبشرط أن يتيح له الفرصة لكي يبدي أسباب معارضته للفسخ ... إذا تغيب العامل دون سبب مشروع أكثر من ثلاثين يوماً خلال السنة العقدية الواحدة أو أكثر من خمسة عشر يوماً متتالية، على أن يسبق الفصل إنذار كتابي من صاحب العمل للعامل بعد غيابه عشرين يوماً في الحالة الأولى وانقطاعه عشرة أيام في الحالة الثانية | The only route to a no-cost dismissal. Procedural conditions are strict: hearing opportunity (Art. 80 chapeau plus Art. 71), the 24-hour report for ground (4), the prior written warning for ground (2), and the 20-day / 10-day warning letters for ground (7). The 2005 body (20 days / 10 consecutive days, warnings at 10 and 5) shown in the scraped file is superseded. Ground (1) now includes assault on a subordinate. A dismissal that fails a condition falls back to Art. 77 plus Art. 76 plus the full end-of-service award | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 81 | The worker may leave without notice, keeping all his statutory rights, if: (1) the employer fails to perform its essential contractual or statutory obligations; (2) the employer or its representative deceived him at contracting about work conditions; (3) he is assigned, without consent, work materially different from that agreed, contrary to Art. 60; (4) the employer, a family member or the responsible manager commits violent assault or immoral conduct towards him or his family; (5) the employer's or manager's treatment is marked by cruelty, injustice or insult; (6) a serious danger to safety or health exists at the workplace which the employer knew of and did not act to remove; (7) the employer's conduct, particularly unjust treatment or breach of contract, drove the worker to appear to be the one terminating | يحق للعامل أن يترك العمل دون إشعار مع احتفاظه بحقوقه النظامية كلها، وذلك في أي من الحالات الآتية: إذا لم يقم صاحب العمل بالوفاء بالتزاماته العقدية أو النظامية الجوهرية إزاء العامل ... إذا كان صاحب العمل أو من يمثله قد دفع العامل بتصرفاته ... إلى أن يكون العامل في الظاهر هو الذي أنهى العقد | Constructive dismissal: the worker keeps the **full** end-of-service award (no Art. 85 fraction) and may claim Art. 77 compensation as the harmed party `[model knowledge — verify]`. Late or unpaid wages (Art. 90) and unpaid Art. 61(5)-(6) allowances are "essential statutory obligations" under (1). HRSD Regs Art. 14 separately allows transfer of a non-Saudi without consent after three months' unpaid wages `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 82 | The employer may not terminate the worker's service on grounds of illness before he has exhausted the sick-leave periods fixed by the Law; the worker may ask to join his annual leave to his sick leave | لا يجوز لصاحب العمل إنهاء خدمة العامل بسبب المرض، قبل استنفاذه المدد المحددة للإجازة المنصوص عليها في هذا النظام، وللعامل الحق في أن يطلب وصل إجازته السنوية بالمرضية | Illness-related termination is possible only after the 120 days of Art. 117 (30 full, 60 at three-quarters, 30 unpaid) in the sick-leave year, plus any annual leave the worker elects to append. Termination for a different legitimate reason during sick leave is not barred by this article's wording but courts scrutinise it `[model knowledge — verify]`. See Art. 155 for pregnancy and maternity | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 83 (M/46) | (1) If the work lets the worker know the employer's clients, the employer may, to protect its legitimate interests, require him not to compete after the contract ends; to be valid the condition must be in writing and specific as to time, place and type of work, and its duration may not exceed two years from the end of the relationship. (2) If the work gives access to the employer's business secrets, the employer may require non-disclosure after the contract ends; to be valid the condition must be in writing and specific as to time, place and type of work. (3) By exception to the Law, the employer may sue within one year from the date of discovering the worker's breach of either obligation | جاز لصاحب العمل – حمايةً لمصالحه المشروعة – أن يشترط على العامل ألا يقوم بعد انتهاء العقد بمنافسته، ويجب لصحة هذا الشرط أن يكون مُحرراً ومُحدداً، من حيث الزمان والمكان ونوع العمل، ويجب ألا تزيد مدته على سنتين من تاريخ انتهاء العلاقة بين الطرفين ... لصاحب العمل رفع دعوى خلال سنة من تاريخ اكتشاف مخالفة العامل | Non-compete drafting: written; named territory; named type of work; term ≤ 2 years; justified by client knowledge or secrets. A non-compete with no geography or no defined activity, or one longer than two years, fails the validity test. Post-termination confidentiality also needs a stated time, place and type of work (an indefinite NDA clause is at risk). The Law does not require consideration or a penalty amount; penalty clauses are commonly used and courts may reduce them `[model knowledge — verify]`. Limitation for the employer's claim: one year from discovery (an exception to Art. 234's 12 months from termination). HRSD Regs Art. 27 extends Art. 83 to flexible-work (العمل المرن) contracts `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 84 (end-of-service award, base formula) | On the end of the employment relationship the employer must pay the worker an award for his period of service computed as half a month's wage for each of the first five years and one month's wage for each of the following years; the last wage is the basis; the worker is entitled to the award for fractions of a year in proportion to the time served | إذا انتهت علاقة العمل وجب على صاحب العمل أن يدفع إلى العامل مكافأة عن مدة خدمته تحسب على أساس أجر نصف شهر عن كل سنة من السنوات الخمس الأولى، وأجر شهر عن كل سنة من السنوات التالية، ويتخذ الأجر الأخير أساساً لحساب المكافأة، ويستحق العامل مكافأة عن أجزاء السنة بنسبة ما قضاه منها في العمل | **Computation inputs.** Wage W = last **actual** monthly wage (Art. 2: basic plus allowances, commissions and customary bonuses; in-kind benefits at up to two months' basic wage per year; commissions and sales percentages may be excluded only by an Art. 86 agreement; piece-rate and commission-only workers use the Art. 96 averages). Service S in years and fractions = continuous service (Art. 2) from the first start date across renewals (Art. 56) and ownership changes (Art. 18), less any unpaid leave beyond 20 days that suspended the contract (Art. 116) `[model knowledge — verify]` on that deduction. Award = 0.5 × W × min(S, 5) + 1.0 × W × max(S − 5, 0). Then apply the Art. 85 fraction if the worker resigned, the Art. 87 exceptions, and the Art. 54 exclusion for probation. Days = months / 30. Unamended since 2005 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 85 and Art. 87 (resignation fractions and exceptions) | If the relationship ends by the worker's resignation, he is entitled to one third of the award after service of not less than two consecutive years and not more than five years, two thirds if his service exceeds five consecutive years but is less than ten, and the full award if his service is ten years or more (Art. 85). By exception to Art. 85, the full award is due if the worker leaves because of force majeure beyond his control, or if a female worker ends the contract within six months of her marriage or three months of giving birth (Art. 87) | إذا كان انتهاء علاقة العمل بسبب استقالة العامل يستحق في هذه الحالة ثلث المكافأة بعد خدمة لا تقل مدتها عن سنتين متتاليتين، ولا تزيد على خمس سنوات، ويستحق ثلثيها إذا زادت مدة خدمته على خمس سنوات متتالية ولم تبلغ عشر سنوات، ويستحق المكافأة كاملة إذا بلغت مدة خدمته عشر سنوات فأكثر ... كما تستحقها العاملة إذا أنهت العقد خلال ستة أشهر من تاريخ عقد زواجها أو ثلاثة أشهر من تاريخ وضعها | **Resignation multiplier on the Art. 84 figure:** S < 2 years: 0; 2 ≤ S ≤ 5 years: 1/3; 5 < S < 10 years: 2/3; S ≥ 10 years: 1. Exactly five years falls in the 1/3 band on the wording ("لا تزيد على خمس سنوات"). Employer-initiated termination, expiry of a fixed term, mutual agreement, Art. 74 events and Art. 81 departures pay the full award; a contract may promise more (Art. 8). Whether an Art. 75 notice termination by the worker on an indefinite contract is a "resignation" for Art. 85, now that Art. 2 defines resignation by reference to fixed-term contracts, is an open question; pre-M/44 practice applied the fractions `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 86 | By exception to Art. 8, it may be agreed that the wage used for the end-of-service award does not include all or some commissions, sales percentages and similar wage elements that by nature rise and fall | استثناء من حكم المادة (الثامنة) من هذا النظام، يجوز الاتفاق على ألا تحسب في الأجر الذي تسوى على أساسه مكافأة نهاية الخدمة جميع مبالغ العمولات أو بعضها والنسب المئوية عن ثمن المبيعات وما أشبه ذلك | Only variable commission-type elements can be excluded, and only by agreement (contract or work regulations). Fixed allowances (housing, transport) cannot be excluded under this article | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 88 | When the worker's service ends the employer must pay his wage and settle his dues within one week at most from the end of the contractual relationship; if the worker ended the contract, the employer must settle his dues in full within no more than two weeks; the employer may deduct any debt owed to it by reason of the work from the amounts due to the worker | إذا انتهت خدمة العامل وجب على صاحب العمل دفع أجره وتصفية حقوقه خلال أسبوع- على الأكثر- من تاريخ انتهاء العلاقة العقدية، أما إذا كان العامل هو الذي أنهى العقد وجب على صاحب العمل تصفية حقوقه كاملة خلال مدة لا تزيد على أسبوعين. ولصاحب العمل أن يحسم أي دين مستحق له بسبب العمل | Final settlement deadline: 7 days (employer-initiated or expiry), 14 days (worker-initiated). Set-off is limited to work-related debts (loans, damage under Art. 91, pay in lieu under Art. 76); it does not permit withholding the award pending return of equipment or clearance unless a quantified debt exists `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 89, 90 (M/46) | The Council of Ministers may set a minimum wage on the Minister's proposal (Art. 89). Wages and all sums due must be paid in the official currency: daily workers at least weekly; monthly workers once a month; piece work exceeding two weeks with weekly instalments and the balance the week after delivery; otherwise at least weekly. Establishments must pay wages into workers' accounts through approved banks in the Kingdom, within those deadlines; the Minister may exempt some establishments (Art. 90) | تلزم المنشآت بدفع الأجور في حسابات العمال عن طريق البنوك المعتمدة في المملكة، بشرط ألا يتجاوز موعد استحقاقها المواعيد المحددة أعلاه، ويجوز للوزير استثناء بعض المنشآت من ذلك | Art. 90(2) is the statutory basis of the Wage Protection System; HRSD monitors bank payment files through the Mudad platform and treats late or partial payment as a violation `[model knowledge — verify]`. The 2005 body ("may pay through banks if the worker agrees") is superseded. No general statutory minimum wage appears in the Law; the SAR 4,000 figure used for counting Saudis in Nitaqat is a programme rule, not Art. 89 `[model knowledge — verify]`. Cash payment or payment to a third party's account is a breach. Three months' non-payment lets a non-Saudi transfer without consent (Regs Art. 14) `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Arts. 91-94 (deductions and late payment) | Damage caused by the worker's fault may be recovered by deduction of up to five days' wage per month, subject to a 15-working-day objection window to the labor dispute body (Art. 91). No deduction for private rights without written consent except: employer loans (deduction capped at 10% of wage), social-insurance and statutory contributions, savings-fund contributions and loans, housing-scheme instalments, disciplinary fines and damage amounts, and judgment debts (capped at one quarter of the wage unless the judgment says otherwise; maintenance first) (Art. 92). Total deductions may not exceed half the wage unless the dispute body allows more, and never beyond three quarters (Art. 93). For an unauthorised deduction or unjustified late payment the worker, his representative or the labor office may apply for an order to refund or pay, and the body may fine the employer up to double the amount deducted or delayed (Art. 94) | لا يجوز حسم أي مبلغ من أجور العامل لقاء حقوق خاصة دون موافقة خطية منه، إلا في الحالات الآتية ... لا يجوز- في جميع الأحوال- أن تزيد نسبة المبالغ المحسومة على نصف أجر العامل المستحق ... أن توقع عليه غرامة لا تتجاوز ضعف ما حسم من أجر العامل أو ضعف قيمة الأجر المتأخر | Payroll deduction rule of thumb: loans ≤10% of wage, disciplinary fines ≤5 days per month, damage ≤5 days per month, all deductions ≤50% of wage. References to the "labor dispute body" (هيئة تسوية الخلافات العمالية) now mean the Labor Courts (M/1 of 1435H) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 95-97 | If the contract or work regulations do not state the wage, the wage for similar work in the establishment applies, else the custom of the profession in the locality, else the dispute body fixes it by equity (Art. 95). For piece or production wages, the average wage of actual working days in the last year of service is used to compute any statutory right; for commission-only or similar wages, the average daily wage is total earnings divided by actual working days (Art. 96). A worker detained by the authorities in a matter connected with the work receives 50% of his wage for up to 180 days; refunded to the worker on acquittal or dismissal of the investigation, retained by the worker on conviction unless the judgment says otherwise (Art. 97) | إذا كان أجر العامل محدداً على أساس القطعة أو الإنتاج يعتمد متوسط الأجر الذي تقاضاه عن أيام عمله الفعلية في السنة الأخيرة من خدمته، لغرض حساب أي من الحقوق المقررة له بموجب هذا النظام | Use the Art. 96 averages as the "wage" input for end-of-service award and Art. 77 compensation for sales staff paid wholly or mainly on commission; the Art. 86 exclusion, if agreed, removes the variable part from the award only | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 98-100 (working hours) | A worker may not actually work more than eight hours a day (daily criterion) or 48 hours a week (weekly criterion); during Ramadan actual hours for Muslims are reduced to a maximum of six hours a day or 36 a week (Art. 98). The Minister may raise the limit to nine hours a day for some categories, industries and non-continuous jobs, or reduce it to seven hours for hazardous or harmful work (Art. 99). With the Ministry's approval, shift-based establishments may exceed eight hours a day or 48 a week provided the average over three weeks or less does not exceed those limits (Art. 100) | لا يجوز تشغيل العامل تشغيلاً فعليًّا أكثر من ثماني ساعات في اليوم الواحد ... أو أكثر من ثمان وأربعين ساعة في الأسبوع ... وتخفض ساعات العمل الفعلية خلال شهر رمضان للمسلمين، بحيث لا تزيد على ست ساعات في اليوم، أو ست وثلاثين ساعة في الأسبوع | The portal does **not** mark Arts. 98, 99, 100 or 104 as amended, and the Arabic body reads 8/48 and 6/36. The Bureau of Experts English appendix nevertheless lists amendments to Arts. 98-100 (9/45, 7/35) and 104 (two rest days) under M/46; the writer found no corresponding pop-up on the portal and follows the Arabic: 8 hours / 48 hours, 6 / 36 in Ramadan, one weekly rest day. Treat the English appendix entries as unverified (see open questions). Ramadan reduction applies to Muslim workers only | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Arts. 101 (M/46), 102, 103 | Hours and rest periods must be arranged so that no worker works more than five consecutive hours without a break of at least half an hour each time for rest, prayer and meals, and so that the worker does not remain at the workplace more than twelve hours a day (Art. 101). Rest, prayer and meal periods are not counted as actual working hours; the worker is not under the employer's authority during them and may not be required to stay at the workplace (Art. 102). The Minister may specify continuous-work cases where breaks are given during work as management organises (Art. 103) | لا يعمل العامل أكثر من خمس ساعات متتالية دون فترة للراحة والصلاة والطعام لا تقل عن نصف ساعة في المرة الواحدة خلال مجموع ساعات العمل، وبحيث لا يبقى العامل في مكان العمل أكثر من اثنتي عشرة ساعة في اليوم الواحد | Maximum presence at the workplace is 12 hours (M/46 raised it from the 11 hours in the scraped body). Unpaid breaks require that the worker is free to leave; a "working lunch" at the desk is working time | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 104-105 (weekly rest) | Friday is the weekly rest day for all workers; after notifying the competent labor office the employer may substitute another day for some workers and must let them perform religious duties; the weekly rest day may not be replaced by cash; it is paid in full and is not less than 24 consecutive hours (Art. 104). In remote areas and continuous-process work, weekly rest may be accumulated for up to eight weeks by agreement and with the Ministry's approval, counted from arrival at the nearest city with transport (Art. 105) | يوم الجمعة يوم الراحة الأسبوعية لجميع العمال ... ولا يجوز تعويض يوم الراحة الأسبوعية بمقابل نقدي. يكون يوم الراحة الأسبوعية بأجر كامل، ولا يقل عن أربع وعشرين ساعة متتالية | The Law guarantees one paid rest day (Friday); the Friday-Saturday weekend is practice and the Regulations' holiday overlap rule refers to "الراحة الأسبوعية" generally `[authority — HRSD]`. Work on the rest day is overtime under Art. 107(3). Substituting Friday requires a labor-office notification | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Arts. 106, 107 (para 1 M/44) (overtime) | The employer may depart from Arts. 98, 101 and 104(1) for annual inventory, budgets, liquidation, closing accounts, sale and season preparation (not more than 30 days a year), preventing or repairing accidents, unusual work pressure, and holidays and seasons fixed by the Minister; in all such cases actual hours may not exceed ten a day or sixty a week; the Minister fixes the annual overtime ceiling (Art. 106). The employer must pay for each overtime hour the hourly wage plus 50% of his basic wage; with the worker's consent the employer may grant paid compensatory leave days instead of overtime pay, as the Regulations provide; on the weekly criterion, hours above the weekly standard are overtime; all hours worked on holidays and Eids are overtime (Art. 107) | يجب على صاحب العمل أن يدفع للعامل أجراً إضافيًّا عن ساعات العمل الإضافية يوازي أجر الساعة مضافاً إليه (50%) من أجره الأساسي، ويجوز لصاحب العمل بموافقة العامل أن يحتسب للعامل أيام إجازة تعويضية مدفوعة الأجر بدلاً عن الأجر المستحق للعامل لساعات العمل الإضافية ... تعد جميع ساعات العمل التي تؤدَّى في أيام العطل والأعياد ساعات إضافية | **Overtime rate = hourly (actual) wage + 50% of the hourly basic wage.** Hourly wage is not defined in the text; practice divides the monthly wage by 30 days and by the daily hours (8) `[model knowledge — verify]`. HRSD Regs Art. 22: annual overtime may not exceed 720 hours, more only with the worker's consent; Regs Art. 22 bis: compensatory leave in lieu requires the worker's consent, must be at least 1.5 hours' leave per overtime hour, is to be taken within 60 days of the overtime unless otherwise agreed, may not exceed 30 days a year, and unused compensatory leave is paid out on leaving `[authority — HRSD]`. Time-off in lieu was not lawful before 19 February 2025 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 108 (exempt categories) | Arts. 98 and 101 do not apply to: (1) persons in senior positions of responsibility in management and direction whose positions give them the employer's authority over workers; (2) preparatory or complementary work before or after the working day; (3) work that is necessarily intermittent; (4) guards and cleaners, other than civil security guards. The Regulations define categories (2)-(4) and their maximum hours | لا تسري أحكام المادتين الثامنة والتسعين والأولى بعد المائة من هذا النظام على الحالات الآتية: الأشخاص الذين يشغلون مناصب عالية ذات مسؤولية في الإدارة والتوجيه، إذا كان من شأن هذه المناصب أن يتمتع شاغلوها بسلطات صاحب العمل على العمال | Senior managers with employer-like authority are outside the hours and break rules and hence outside overtime; a job title alone does not qualify, the test is authority over workers. HRSD Regs Art. 23: preparatory/complementary work is capped at 30 minutes a day (15 + 15); intermittent work at 10 hours a day (8 in Ramadan) and 48 hours a week (36 in Ramadan); guards and cleaners at 12 hours a day (10 in Ramadan) and 48 a week; hours beyond those are overtime `[authority — HRSD]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Arts. 109-111 (annual leave) | Annual leave of not less than 21 days a year, rising to not less than 30 days once the worker has completed five consecutive years with the employer, paid in advance; the worker must take the leave in the year it falls due and may not waive it or take cash in lieu during service; the employer fixes the dates according to work requirements or by rotation and must notify the worker at least 30 days in advance (Art. 109). With the employer's consent the worker may carry leave to the following year; the employer may defer leave beyond its year for up to 90 days for work reasons, and further only with the worker's written consent and not beyond the end of the following year (Art. 110). A worker who leaves before taking due leave is paid for the untaken days and for fractions of a year pro rata (Art. 111) | يستحق العامل عن كل عام إجازة سنوية لا تقل مدتها عن واحد وعشرين يوماً، تُزاد إلى مدة لا تقل عن ثلاثين يوماً إذا أمضى العامل في خدمة صاحب العمل خمس سنوات متصلة، وتكون الإجازة بأجر يدفع مقدماً ... ولا يجوز النزول عنها، أو أن يتقاضى بدلاً نقدياً عوضاً عن الحصول عليها أثناء خدمته ... للعامل الحق في الحصول على أجرة عن أيام الإجازة المستحقة إذا ترك العمل قبل استعماله لها | Leave-tracker rules: accrual 21 calendar days (30 after five years) per year of service, pro rata for partial years; the Law counts days, not working days, and does not say whether weekly rest days inside a leave block count, so the work regulations should say `[model knowledge — verify]`. "Use it or lose it" policies are invalid: untaken leave is paid at termination (Art. 111) and cannot be forfeited; carry-over beyond the following year requires the worker's written consent. Unamended since 2005 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 112 (official holidays) | Every worker is entitled to full-pay leave on the Eids and occasions specified by the Regulations | لكل عامل الحق في إجازة بأجر كامل في الأعياد والمناسبات التي تحددها اللائحة | HRSD Regs Art. 24: Eid al-Fitr 4 days from the day after 29 Ramadan (Umm al-Qura); Eid al-Adha 4 days from the Day of Arafah; National Day 1 day (first day of Libra, Umm al-Qura, i.e. 23 September); Founding Day 1 day (22 February). Overlap: with weekly rest, the worker gets an equivalent day before or after; with annual leave, the annual leave is extended by those days; with sick leave, full pay for the holiday days; National Day or Founding Day falling inside an Eid holiday is not compensated `[authority — HRSD]`. Hours worked on these days are overtime (Art. 107(3)) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 113 (M/44) (marriage, bereavement, paternity) | Taking into account female workers' leaves under the Law, a worker is entitled to full-pay leave of five days on his marriage or on the death of his spouse or an ascendant or descendant, and three days on the death of a brother or sister, all counted from the date of the event; and three days on the birth of a child, to be taken within seven days of the birth; the employer may request supporting documents | للعامل الحق في إجازة بأجر كامل لمدة (خمسة) أيام عند زواجه، أو في حالة وفاة زوجه أو أحد أصوله أو فروعه، و(ثلاثة) أيام في حالة وفاة الأخ أو الأخت؛ تحتسب جميعها من تاريخ الواقعة. و(ثلاثة) أيام في حالة ولادة مولود له خلال (سبعة) أيام من تاريخ الولادة | New since 19 February 2025: sibling bereavement leave (3 days) and a 7-day window for paternity leave. Spouse bereavement was already 5 days under M/46; the "new bereavement leave for a spouse" mentioned in briefing notes refers to Art. 160 iddah leave for widows (below), not to Art. 113. Leave runs from the event date (calendar days), so an event on a rest day consumes the entitlement. The 2005 body (1 day birth, 3 days marriage or death) is superseded | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 114 (Hajj) and Art. 115 (M/46) (exams) | Paid Hajj leave of not less than 10 and not more than 15 days including the Eid al-Adha holiday, once during service, if the worker has not performed Hajj before and has completed at least two consecutive years with the employer; the employer may fix the number of workers granted this leave each year (Art. 114). Exam leave: with the employer's approval of enrolment, full-pay leave for the actual exam days of a non-repeated year, unpaid for a repeated year, forfeited with disciplinary exposure if the worker did not sit; without approval, exam days come out of annual leave or are unpaid; 15 days' advance application; supporting documents on request (Art. 115) | للعامل الحق في الحصول على إجازة بأجر لا تقل مدتها عن عشرة أيام ولا تزيد على خمسة عشر يوماً بما فيها إجازة عيد الأضحى؛ وذلك لأداء فريضة الحج لمرة واحدة طوال مدة خدمته إذا لم يكن قد أداها من قبل | Hajj leave is a once-per-employment entitlement conditional on two years' service and prior non-performance; the employer controls annual quotas but not eligibility | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Art. 116 (unpaid leave) | With the employer's consent the worker may take unpaid leave of an agreed length; the contract is suspended for the portion exceeding 20 days unless the parties agree otherwise | يجوز للعامل بموافقة صاحب العمل الحصول على إجازة دون أجر، يتفق الطرفان على تحديد مدتها، ويعد عقد العمل موقوفاً خلال مدة الإجازة فيما زاد على عشرين يوماً، ما لم يتفق الطرفان على خلاف ذلك | HRSD Regs Art. 25: once suspended, all effects of the contract are suspended unless otherwise agreed, and a fixed-term contract is extended by the suspension period unless otherwise agreed `[authority — HRSD]`. Suspension beyond 20 days therefore stops service accrual for end-of-service award and annual leave unless the parties agree otherwise `[model knowledge — verify]` on the accrual consequence | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Art. 117 (sick leave) and Art. 118 | A worker whose illness is proven is entitled to sick leave at full pay for the first 30 days, three-quarters pay for the next 60 days and without pay for the following 30 days in a single year, whether continuous or intermittent; the "year" starts on the date of the first sick leave (Art. 117). A worker on any leave under this chapter may not work for another employer; if he does, the employer may withhold or recover the leave pay (Art. 118) | للعامل الذي يثبت مرضه الحق في إجازة مرضية بأجر عن الثلاثين يوماً الأولى، وبثلاثة أرباع الأجر عن الستين يوماً التالية، ودون أجر للثلاثين يوماً التي تلي ذلك خلال السنة الواحدة ... ويقصد بالسنة الواحدة: السنة التي تبدأ من تاريخ أول إجازة مرضية | Sick-leave year is a rolling year from the first sick day, not the calendar or contract year. Tiers: days 1-30 at 100%, 31-90 at 75%, 91-120 at 0%. HRSD Regs Art. 26: sick days during annual leave suspend the annual leave, which resumes afterwards; weekly rest days during sick leave are not compensated `[authority — HRSD]`. Termination for illness is barred until the 120 days are exhausted (Art. 82) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Arts. 119-120 (part-time and flexible work) | Full-time workers subject to a collective temporary reduction of hours for economic, technical or structural reasons are not part-time workers (Art. 119). The Minister issues the rules for part-time work and the obligations of each party; the Law applies to part-time workers only to the extent the Minister decides, except the safety, occupational health and work-injury protection of comparable full-time workers (Art. 120) | يصدر الوزير القواعد والضوابط اللازمة لتنظيم العمل لبعض الوقت ... ولا تسري أحكام هذا النظام إلا في الحدود التي يقررها الوزير، باستثناء الحماية الممنوحة للعاملين طوال الوقت المماثلين، من حيث السلامة والصحة المهنية وإصابات العمل | HRSD Regs Art. 27: a part-time contract must be written, fixed-term, and state hours below half the establishment's normal daily hours; part-timers get leave, weekly rest, holidays and overtime rules; unjustified early termination gives the harmed party the wages for the rest of the term unless otherwise agreed. Flexible work (العمل المرن): Saudis only, hourly pay, under half the establishment's hours with one employer, no paid leave, no end-of-service award, no probation, contract ≤ 1 year with one employer, hours above 95 a month are overtime and above 160 a month prohibited, contract must be documented on the Ministry portal, Art. 83 non-compete applies `[authority — HRSD]`. Worker-classification skills should treat flexible-work contracts as a distinct statutory category, not as contractors | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Arts. 121-125 (occupational safety duties) | The employer must keep the establishment clean and healthy, lit, with drinking and washing water, and meet the safety standards set by the Minister (Art. 121); must take the precautions needed to protect workers from work hazards and diseases and post safety instructions in Arabic and any language the workers understand, and may not charge workers for this protection (Art. 122); must inform the worker before starting work of the risks of his occupation, require use of the prescribed protective means, and provide and train on personal protective equipment (Art. 123). The worker must use the protective means, follow the instructions and not misuse or disable them (Art. 124). The employer must take fire precautions, provide fire-fighting means and escape routes, and post fire instructions (Art. 125) | على كل صاحب عمل أن يتخذ الاحتياطات اللازمة لحماية العمال من الأخطار والأمراض الناجمة عن العمل ... وأن يعلن في مكان ظاهر في المنشأة التعليمات الخاصة بسلامة العمل والعمال، وذلك باللغة العربية وبأي لغة أخرى يفهمها العمال ... ولا يجوز لصاحب العمل أن يحمّل العمال أو يقتطع من أجورهم أي مبلغ لقاء توفير هذه الحماية | For a termination review: a dismissal under Art. 80(2) for breach of safety instructions requires that the instructions were posted in a visible place (Art. 122) and a prior written warning. For a hiring review: PPE and safety induction are statutory, not policy, obligations; deductions for PPE are prohibited. Art. 131 bis (M/5) lets the Minister list hazardous occupations and the categories barred from them | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 132-139 (work injuries; Art. 137 M/46) | This chapter does not apply to establishments covered by the occupational-hazards branch of the Social Insurance Law (Art. 132). Otherwise the employer must treat a work injury or occupational disease and bear all costs (Art. 133); injury is defined by reference to the Social Insurance Law, occupational diseases count as work injuries, and the first medical observation is the injury date (Art. 134); relapse or complication is treated as the injury (Art. 135); temporary disability: financial aid equal to the full wage for 60 days then 75% of the wage for the duration of treatment; after one year, or if recovery is medically improbable, the injury is deemed total disability, the contract ends and the worker is compensated, with no recovery of amounts paid in that year (Art. 137); permanent total disability or death: compensation equal to three years' wage, minimum SAR 54,000; permanent partial disability: the disability percentage of that amount (Art. 138); no liability where the worker intentionally injured himself, the injury resulted from his deliberate misconduct, or he refused examination or treatment without legitimate reason (Art. 139) | إذا أصيب العامل بإصابة عمل، أو بمرض مهني، فإن صاحب العمل يلتزم بعلاجه، ويتحمل جميع النفقات اللازمة لذلك ... للمصاب - في حالة عجزه المؤقت عن العمل الناتج من إصابة عمل - الحق في معونة مالية تعادل أجره كاملاً لمدة ستين يوماً ثم يستحق مقابلاً ماليًّا يعادل (75%) من أجره طوال المدة التي يستغرقها علاجه ... تعويض يقدر بما يعادل أجره عن مدة ثلاث سنوات بحد أدنى قدره أربعة وخمسون ألف ريال | Most private-sector workers are GOSI-registered, so Art. 132 routes work-injury benefits to GOSI's occupational-hazards branch and these articles apply only where GOSI does not `[model knowledge — verify]`. Termination for a work injury before the one-year mark, or before medical determination of total disability, is not an Art. 137 termination. The 30-day full-wage period in the scraped body is superseded by 60 days (M/46) | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 149-150 (deleted by M/5), 151 (M/44), 153-154 (women: hazardous work, maternity, medical care, nursing) | Arts. 149 (women barred from hazardous work) and 150 (no night work for women) were deleted by M/5 of 1442H. Maternity leave (Art. 151): a female worker is entitled to fully paid maternity leave of 12 weeks, of which the six weeks after delivery are compulsory; she may distribute the remaining six weeks as she sees fit starting four weeks before the expected delivery date fixed by a certified medical certificate; if less than six weeks remain because delivery is later than expected, the period completing the six weeks is unpaid; in all cases she may extend the leave by one month without pay. Where the child is sick or has a disability requiring constant care, she is entitled to one further month at full pay after the maternity leave, extendable by one unpaid month. The employer must provide medical care during pregnancy and delivery (Art. 153). On return, nursing breaks totalling up to one hour a day, counted as working time without wage reduction, in addition to ordinary breaks (Art. 154) | للمرأة العاملة الحق في إجازة وضع بأجر كامل لمدة (اثني عشر) أسبوعاً، منها وجوبيًّا الأسابيع الستة التالية للوضع، ولها أن توزع الأسابيع الستة المتبقية وفق ما تراه، ابتداءً من أربعة أسابيع قبل التاريخ المرجح للوضع ... وفي جميع الأحوال يحق للمرأة العاملة تمديد هذه الإجازة (شهراً) دون أجر | Leave-tracker rules since 19 February 2025: 12 weeks at full pay regardless of length of service (the service-based half-pay/full-pay scale of the 2005 Art. 152 in the scraped body was abolished by M/46 and Art. 152 merged into 151); at most 4 weeks may be taken before the due date; 6 weeks after delivery are mandatory (no work permitted); optional unpaid extension of one month; plus one paid month for a sick or disabled child. Women may now work at night and in any lawful occupation subject to the Minister's hazardous-work lists under Art. 131 bis. Art. 87 gives a full end-of-service award to a woman who resigns within three months of delivery | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 155 (M/134), 156 (repealed), 157 | The employer may not dismiss a female worker, or warn her of dismissal, during her pregnancy or maternity leave, including any period of illness resulting from either, proven by an approved medical certificate, provided her absence does not exceed 180 days a year, continuous or intermittent (Art. 155). Art. 156 was repealed by M/134. A female worker forfeits her entitlements under this Part if she works for another employer during her authorised leave, and the original employer may withhold or recover her leave pay (Art. 157) | لا يجوز لصاحب العمل فصل العاملة أو إنذارها بالفصل أثناء حملها أو تمتعها بإجازة الوضع، ويشمل ذلك مدة مرضها الناشئ عن أي منهما، على أن يثبت المرض بشهادة طبية معتمدة، وأن لا تتجاوز مدة غيابها (مائة وثمانين) يوماً في السنة | Protected period covers the entire pregnancy (from its start, not from notification) through maternity leave and related illness up to 180 days' absence a year; a dismissal notice served in that window is itself prohibited. The 2005 text's separate 180-day pre-delivery protection (old Art. 156) is repealed and folded into Art. 155. Termination for an Art. 80 ground during pregnancy is not expressly excepted; treat as high risk `[model knowledge — verify]` | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 158-160 (Art. 160 M/46) | Seats for rest for women in all workplaces (Art. 158). An employer with 50 or more female workers must provide a suitable place with enough nannies for workers' children under six where there are ten or more such children; the Minister may require an employer with 100 or more women in one city to set up or contract with a nursery and fix the cost share (Art. 159). Iddah leave (Art. 160): a Muslim female worker whose husband dies is entitled to full-pay iddah leave of not less than four months and ten days from the date of death, extendable without pay if she is pregnant until delivery, after which the remainder lapses; a non-Muslim female worker is entitled to 15 days' full-pay leave; in all cases she may not work for others during the leave; supporting documents on request | للمرأة العاملة المسلمة التي يتوفى زوجها الحق في إجازة عدة بأجر كامل لمدة لا تقل عن أربعة أشهر وعشرة أيام من تاريخ الوفاة ... للمرأة العاملة غير المسلمة التي يتوفى زوجها الحق في إجازة بأجر كامل لمدة خمسة عشر يوماً | This is the spouse-bereavement leave for widows: 130 days at full pay (Muslim) or 15 days (non-Muslim), in addition to nothing else under Art. 113 (Art. 113 opens "taking into account female workers' leaves"). A widower's leave is the 5 days under Art. 113. The 15-day rule in the scraped body applied to all women before M/46 | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 161-167 (minors) | Minors (15 to under 18, Art. 2) may not work in hazardous or harmful work or in occupations likely to endanger their health, safety or morals, as listed by the Minister (Art. 161). No one under 15 may be employed or admitted to workplaces; the Minister may raise this age and may permit light work for ages 13-15 that is not harmful to health or development and does not impair schooling or training (Art. 162). No night work for minors during a period of at least 12 consecutive hours except as the Minister decides (Art. 163). Actual working hours for minors: not more than six a day (four in Ramadan); not more than four consecutive hours without a break of at least half an hour; not more than seven hours at the workplace; no work on weekly rest days, holidays or annual leave; the Art. 106 exceptions do not apply (Art. 164). Before employing a minor the employer must obtain and file the national ID or birth certificate, a certified medical fitness certificate and the guardian's consent (Art. 165); must notify the labor office within the first week and keep a minors' register (Art. 166). The Part does not apply to school or training work, or to persons aged at least 14 in approved educational or training programmes on the Minister's conditions (Art. 167) | لا يجوز تشغيل أي شخص لم يتم الخامسة عشرة من عمره ولا يسمح له بدخول أماكن العمل ... لا يجوز تشغيل الأحداث تشغيلاً فعليًّا أكثر من ست ساعات في اليوم الواحد لسائر شهور السنة، عدا شهر رمضان، فيجب ألا تزيد ساعات العمل الفعلية فيه على أربع ساعات | Hiring-review checklist for anyone under 18: age proof, medical certificate, guardian consent, labor-office notification, register entry, six-hour day, no night or overtime work. HRSD Regs Art. 33 confirms no one under 15 may be employed except under Art. 167; Regs Art. 34 permits night work for minors in family-only establishments, vocational schools, bakeries (except 9 pm to 4 am) and emergencies `[authority — HRSD]`. Art. 186 (M/5) bars anyone under 18 from mines and quarries | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |
| Part 14 (Arts. 210-228) repealed by M/1 of 1435H; Arts. 234-235 (M/14) | Part 14 (Commissions for Settlement of Labor Disputes, including the old Art. 222 limitation and Art. 224 arbitration clause) was repealed by Royal Decree M/1 of 22/1/1435H, and every reference to the Commissions is replaced by "Labor Court(s)" from the establishment of the Labor Courts. Art. 234: (a) the Labor Courts may not hear any claim for a right under this Law or arising from an employment contract after 12 months from the end of the employment relationship, unless the claimant presents an excuse accepted by the court or the defendant acknowledges the right; (b) labor cases are heard urgently. Art. 235: during a case before the Labor Courts the employer may not change the working conditions in force before the proceedings in a way that prejudices the worker's position in the case | تم إلغاء الباب الرابع عشر بموجب المرسوم الملكي (م/1) وتاريخ 22/1/1435هـ. أ- لا تقبل أمام المحاكم العمالية أي دعوى تتعلق بالمطالبة بحق من الحقوق المنصوص عليها في هذا النظام أو الناشئة عن عقد العمل بعد مضي اثني عشر شهراً من تاريخ انتهاء علاقة العمل، ما لم يقدم المدعي عذراً تقبله المحكمة، أو يصدر من المدعى عليه إقرار بالحق. ب- تنظر الدعاوى العمالية على وجه الاستعجال | **Limitation: 12 months from the end of the relationship (Art. 234, not Art. 222).** Exceptions: court-accepted excuse, or the defendant's acknowledgment of the right; Art. 83(3) gives the employer one year from discovery for non-compete and confidentiality claims. The Labor Courts sit within the Ministry of Justice under the Judiciary Law; a claim is filed after a mandatory amicable-settlement attempt at the HRSD labor office (the "friendly settlement" step of old Art. 220 survives as a procedural rule under the Labor Courts' procedures, not as an article of this Law) `[model knowledge — verify]`. Whether an arbitration clause in an employment contract is enforceable after the repeal of Art. 224 is an open question `[model knowledge — verify]`. Art. 235 freezes pay and conditions once a claim is filed | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` |
| Arts. 229 (M/46), 229 bis (M/44), 230 (M/46; para 5 M/44), 231-233 (penalties, note only) | Without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law, a breach of the Law, its Regulations or implementing decisions is punished by one or more of: a fine not exceeding SAR 100,000; closure of the establishment for up to 30 days; permanent closure; the penalty may be doubled for repetition and fines are multiplied by the number of persons concerned (Art. 229). Unlicensed recruitment, Saudi-placement or assignment (Art. 30(1)) is fined between SAR 200,000 and SAR 500,000 (Art. 229 bis). The Ministry may impose fine and temporary closure up to half the maximum by decision of the Minister or delegate, appealable to the administrative court; violations above that or warranting permanent closure go to the competent court on the Ministry's suit; the Ministry publishes violation-and-penalty schedules and may settle a violation for a fine assessed by the Ministry by decision of the Minister or delegate (Art. 230). The violator must rectify within the period fixed by the Regulations, failing which it is a new violation (Art. 231); fines go to the Human Resources Development Fund (Art. 232); the Minister may reward informants up to 25% of a collected fine (Art. 233) | يعاقب كل من يخل بأي حكم من أحكام هذا النظام أو لائحته أو القرارات الصادرة تنفيذا له، بعقوبة أو أكثر من العقوبات الآتية: غرامة مالية لا تتجاوز مائة ألف ريـال. إغلاق المنشأة لمدة لا تزيد على ثلاثين يوماً. إغلاق المنشأة نهائيًّا ... يعاقب بغرامة لا تقل عن (200,000) مائتي ألف ريال ولا تزيد على (500,000) خمسمائة ألف ريال؛ كل من يخالف أحكام الفقرة (1) من المادة (الثلاثين) | Note only: the 2005 penalty articles (Arts. 230-242 with their small fixed fines) shown in the scraped body are consolidated into Arts. 229-233 and the fines in the scraped body are obsolete. HRSD Regs Art. 39: rectification within ten working days of the penalty, otherwise it counts as repetition and the penalty doubles; Regs Art. 38: objection to an administrative penalty within 60 days, settlement application within 90 days; Regs Art. 40: fines payable within 60 days, otherwise Ministry services are suspended `[authority — HRSD]`. Per-worker multiplication makes wage-protection and documentation violations scale with headcount | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[BOE — official English]` `[authority — HRSD]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- **Working hours discrepancy.** The Bureau of Experts English appendix lists M/46 amendments to Arts. 98, 99, 100 (9 hours / 45 hours; 7 / 35 in Ramadan) and 104 (two paid rest days including Friday), but the portal does not mark those articles as amended and the Arabic body reads 8 / 48, 6 / 36 and a single Friday rest day. This file follows the Arabic. Counsel should confirm whether those provisions were ever enacted (the writer believes they were not) and whether any ministerial decision under Art. 99 changes the position for particular sectors.
- **Art. 77 fractional years.** The compensation for terminating an indefinite contract without a legitimate reason is "fifteen days' wage for each year of the worker's service". The text does not say whether partial years are pro-rated (as Art. 84 expressly does for the award) or ignored. Court practice is needed before a skill computes a number for a worker with, say, 3 years and 7 months.
- **Art. 77 contractual compensation below the statutory formula.** Art. 77 opens "unless the contract includes a specific compensation". Whether a contractual figure lower than 15 days per year or lower than the two-month floor survives Art. 8 (conditions less favourable than the Law are void) is not settled by the text.
- **Which wage for Art. 77.** Art. 77 says "wage" (actual wage, Art. 2) but does not say "last wage" as Art. 84 does. Whether courts use the last wage or an average is a practice point.
- **Resignation from an indefinite contract and the Art. 85 fractions.** M/44 defines "resignation" as the written wish to terminate a **fixed-term** contract, and Art. 74 now lists resignation (3 bis) separately from termination at will in indefinite contracts (3). Whether a worker who terminates an indefinite contract on 30 days' notice under Art. 75(1)(a) still receives only the Art. 85 fraction of the end-of-service award, or the full award, is unresolved on the text; pre-M/44 practice applied the fractions to any worker-initiated termination.
- **Art. 79 bis and Art. 77(2).** The defined "resignation" is "without any conditions or restrictions" and the resigning worker "is entitled to all rights under this Law". Whether an employer can still claim Art. 77(2) compensation (wages for the remaining term) from a fixed-term worker who resigns and is deemed accepted by silence needs confirmation.
- **Art. 75 and "legitimate reason".** The Law does not define سبب مشروع. Whether redundancy, restructuring, poor performance without prior warnings, or loss of a client contract are accepted as legitimate reasons, and whether an employer terminating on 60 days' notice with a stated reason is still exposed to Art. 77 if the court rejects the reason, is court practice.
- **Art. 61(5)-(6) allowances.** No amount or benchmark for "appropriate" housing and transport allowances is given, and it is not stated whether an all-inclusive salary already covers them. Whether these allowances form part of the wage for end-of-service and compensation purposes (the text says they are paid "with the wage") is a practice point.
- **Retirement age.** Art. 74(4) now refers to "the retirement age under the Social Insurance Law". The Social Insurance Law and its 2024 amendments were not supplied; the applicable age (and any transitional schedule) must be taken from that instrument.
- **Art. 11 joint liability.** M/46 removed the joint-and-several liability of principal and contractor. Whether courts nevertheless reach the principal (for example under civil-law principles or the Regulations on assignment) is a practice point relevant to outsourcing reviews.
- **Non-Saudi transfer after 12 months (Regs Art. 14, item 21).** The Regulations allow a non-Saudi to move to another employer after 12 months from entry on 90 days' notice "with due regard to Art. 77". How Art. 77(2) compensation is applied in that case, and whether Nitaqat conditions restrict it, needs confirmation.
- **Dispute route details.** The mandatory amicable-settlement step, the Labor Courts' procedural rules, and the enforceability of arbitration clauses after the repeal of Art. 224 are outside the text supplied.
- **GOSI and work injuries.** Arts. 133-139 apply only where the GOSI occupational-hazards branch does not (Art. 132). Which categories of workers fall outside GOSI coverage should be confirmed before applying the Art. 137-138 amounts.
- **Domestic workers and the Art. 7 categories.** The separate HRSD regulations for domestic workers, agricultural workers and short-visit non-Saudis were not supplied; hiring reviews for those categories cannot rely on this file.
- **Regulations not read.** HRSD implementing provisions on Arts. 84-88 (award), 90 (wage-protection mechanics and exemptions), 109-111 (leave accounting), 151 (maternity documentation) and 74-77 do not appear in the April 2025 Implementing Regulations read for this file; ministerial decisions on those points (including the Wage Protection System decision and the violations-and-penalties schedules) were not supplied.

## How skills should use this file

- **Loading.** Employment skills load this file at the step where they identify the governing jurisdiction as `ksa` and before any finding is written: `termination-review` (Step "identify governing law and contract type"), `hiring-review` (Step "statutory requirements checklist"), `worker-classification` (Step "apply the jurisdiction's test"), `wage-hour-qa` (Step "retrieve the statutory rule"), `policy-drafting` and `handbook-updates` (Step "statutory floor for each policy topic"), `log-leave` and the `leave-tracker` agent (Step "look up the leave type's statutory entitlement and deadlines"), `internal-investigation` (Step "procedural requirements"). Corporate skills `diligence-issue-extraction` and `material-contract-schedule` load it when reviewing employment contracts, and `integration-management` when planning a transfer of employees (Art. 18 row). Every finding must cite the row's article number and carry the row's tag verbatim.
- **termination-review** must cite, in order: Art. 74 (termination event), Art. 37 or Art. 55 (contract type), Art. 75 (notice: employer 60 days, worker 30 days for monthly-paid indefinite contracts; 30 days otherwise), Art. 76 (pay in lieu), Art. 80 (grounds for no-cost dismissal and their procedural conditions), Arts. 71-72 (hearing and grievance), Art. 77 (compensation), Arts. 84-88 (award and settlement timing), Art. 82, Art. 155 and Art. 234 (protected periods and limitation), and Art. 83 (post-termination restraints). It must not cite reinstatement under Art. 78; that remedy was repealed by M/46.
- **hiring-review** must cite Arts. 3 and 61(4) (non-discrimination), Arts. 33, 36, 38 (permit and reserved professions for non-Saudis), Art. 37 (non-Saudi contracts fixed-term, default one year), Art. 40 (employer bears fees), Arts. 51-52 (written, documented, model contract), Art. 53-54 (probation ≤ 180 days, once), Art. 61(5)-(6) (housing and transport), Art. 9 (Arabic governs), Arts. 161-167 (minors), and the Art. 7 exclusions.
- **worker-classification** must cite Art. 2 (worker definition), Art. 5(1) (subordination test), Art. 6 and the Art. 2 definitions of temporary, casual, seasonal and part-time work, Arts. 119-120 with HRSD Regs Art. 27 (part-time and flexible work), and Art. 7 (excluded categories). Assignment (الإسناد) under Art. 2 and Art. 30 is a licensed activity; a skill must flag unlicensed staff-supply arrangements against Art. 229 bis.
- **wage-hour-qa** must cite Art. 2 (wage definitions), Arts. 89-97 (payment, WPS, deductions), Arts. 98-108 (hours, Ramadan, breaks, weekly rest, overtime, exemptions) with the HRSD Regs Arts. 22, 22 bis and 23 points in the Practical effect column, and must state the overtime formula as "hourly wage plus 50% of hourly basic wage" and the 720-hour annual cap with its `[authority — HRSD]` tag.
- **policy-drafting** and **handbook-updates** must cite Arts. 12-13 (work regulations must follow the HRSD model and be approved), Arts. 66-73 (closed list of penalties and procedure), Arts. 109-118 and 151-160 (leave floors), Art. 83 (restraint limits), Art. 8 (floors cannot be lowered), and Art. 9 (Arabic version governs). Any policy provision below a row's floor must be flagged as void under Art. 8.
- **log-leave** and **leave-tracker** must map leave types to rows: annual (Arts. 109-111), official holidays (Art. 112 with Regs Art. 24 dates), marriage/bereavement/paternity (Art. 113: 5 days, 3 days sibling, 3 days birth within 7 days), Hajj (Art. 114), exams (Art. 115), unpaid (Art. 116, suspension after 20 days), sick (Art. 117: 30 / 60 at 75% / 30 unpaid per rolling year), maternity (Art. 151: 12 weeks, 6 mandatory after delivery, up to 4 before, plus 1 unpaid month, plus 1 paid month for a sick or disabled child), iddah (Art. 160: 4 months 10 days or 15 days), and must set decision-point alerts at: day 30 and day 90 of the sick-leave year (pay-tier changes), day 120 (Art. 82 termination bar lifts), day 180 of pregnancy-related absence (Art. 155 protection limit), and the end of the following year for deferred annual leave (Art. 110).
- **Computed numbers.** When a skill computes an end-of-service award it must show: last actual monthly wage W (state which components were included and whether an Art. 86 exclusion applies), continuous service S in years and days (start date, end date, calendar used per Art. 10), the Art. 84 formula 0.5 × W × min(S, 5) + W × max(S − 5, 0), the Art. 85 multiplier (0, 1/3, 2/3 or 1) with the triggering event, and any Art. 87 or Art. 54 override, then attach the tag `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` to the formula and `[model knowledge — verify]` to any input the skill inferred (for example whether an allowance is part of the wage). When it computes Art. 77 compensation it must show contract type, W, S or months remaining, the 15-days-per-year or remaining-term amount, the two-month floor, and must flag the fractional-year and contractual-compensation open questions; the result carries `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for the formula and `[model knowledge — verify]` for the fractional-year treatment. Pay in lieu of notice (Art. 76) is W × notice days / 30.
- **Stop rules.** If a skill needs a rule that has no row here (for example the Domestic Workers Regulation, the Social Insurance Law retirement age, the Wage Protection System decision, the violations schedule, Labor Court procedure, or a sector-specific hours decision under Art. 99), it must say that the rule is not in the reference file and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must not fill the gap from memory. If the row it relies on, or the specific sentence within a row, is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, the skill must reproduce that tag on its finding and mark the finding as requiring verification. If a skill detects that the contract or policy under review was signed before 19 February 2025 and the question turns on an M/44 change (Arts. 7, 37, 51, 53, 61(4)-(6), 72, 74(3 bis)/(7 bis), 75, 79 bis, 107(1), 113, 151), it must note that the pre-amendment text applied at signing and that the amended text now governs, and flag transitional treatment for counsel.

