FILE: platform-obligations
 Labor Law Art. 51 (as amended by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 18(2)  |  The employment contract must be written in two copies, one kept by each party, and must be documented in accordance with the relevant statutory provisions and as the Regulation sets out; the contract exists even if unwritten, in which case the worker alone may prove it and his rights by all means of proof, and either party may demand a written contract at any time. Regulation Art. 18(2): the employer must document workers' contracts electronically through the ministry's approved platform.  |  Qiwa authentication of every employee's contract is a legal duty on the employer, not a convenience: the amended Art. 51 says "يجب توثيقه" and the Regulation names the platform. An undocumented contract remains valid and enforceable against the employer (the worker can prove it by any means); the sanction falls on the employer: Art. 229 (fine up to SAR 100,000 per person, closure) applied through the Minister's schedule under Art. 230 (the current schedule amount is `[model knowledge — verify]`), and loss of Nitaqat credit for undocumented Saudi contracts from April 2026 `[model knowledge — verify]`, see `saudization-nitaqat.md`.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 51; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 18 
 Labor Law Art. 40 (para (1) as reworded by M/44 of 1446H)  |  The employer bears the non-Saudi worker's recruitment fees, iqama and work-permit fees and their renewal, the fines for delay in those that the employer causes, occupation-change fees, exit and re-entry fees, and the return ticket after the relationship ends; the worker bears his return cost if unfit for work or if he wishes to return without a legitimate reason. The employer bears the transfer-of-services fee for a worker who wishes to transfer to it. The employer bears the cost of preparing and repatriating a deceased worker's body unless GOSI does.  |  Every platform fee in this file (Qiwa work permit and levy, Muqeem iqama, Absher exit/re-entry, transfer fee) is by law an employer cost; deduction from the worker's wage or clawback at exit is unlawful, and clauses purporting to do so are a standard hiring-review and termination-review finding. The M/44 rewording limits the employer's liability for delay fines to those "caused by the employer", which opens a narrow argument that a fine caused by the worker (for example, failing to present for iqama renewal) may be charged to him; the only other costs the worker may bear are his own return ticket in the two Art. 40(2) cases.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 Second items 1, 11, 14, 15, 16, 17 (transfer of services with consent)  |  A non-Saudi worker may move to another employer without any minimum service period if the current employer consents, unless Nitaqat provides otherwise. Transfer requests are submitted electronically through the ministry's approved platform. Both the former and the new employer must keep the worker's written consent to the transfer before the procedure. The current employer's consent must be given electronically on the transfer request or by a paper waiver attested by an official body addressed to the requesting employer, and may not be made subject to any condition; the consent is not required in the no-consent cases below or for workers moving to a new contractor under continuing government operation, maintenance and cleaning contracts. If the transfer is not approved after the consent was given, the worker stays with the original employer. No decision or final judgment inconsistent with the transfer may exist before it is completed.  |  The transfer runs on Qiwa: the receiving employer requests, the current employer accepts electronically, the worker accepts. "May not be conditioned" bars the practice of demanding payment or a release as the price of a Qiwa release; a clause in a settlement agreement conditioning the transfer approval on a waiver is unenforceable as a condition of the approval itself `[model knowledge — verify]` for court practice. Keep the worker's written consent on file (item 14).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 Second items 5, 6, 7, 8, 18, 21 (transfer without the employer's consent)  |  A non-Saudi may transfer without the current employer's consent: (5) as a newly arrived worker for whom no work permit has been issued; (6) where his work permit or iqama has expired; (7) on expiry of the documented employment contract (subject to item 21); (8) by decision of the Minister or delegate where the employer prolongs a pending court case (absence from two hearings without accepted excuse, or proven delay), on the court's recommendation, where the establishment has not paid three consecutive months' wages or is late past the third month's due date (application within one Hijri year), where the employer is absent (travel, imprisonment, death or otherwise) leaving the establishment unable to meet its obligations for three months without an appointed representative, for categories or nationalities with special instructions on expiry or employer termination, for an accompanying dependant after the end of the relationship, for spouse reunification, where the worker reports commercial concealment with evidence, or in any other public-interest case the Minister decides. If a transfer in an item (7) case is not approved, the Minister may license the worker to work for another employer. (21) Subject to Art. 77, a non-Saudi may move without consent once he has completed twelve months from entry and has given the current employer not less than ninety days' notice before ending the relationship, unless otherwise agreed. Item 2 bars transfers to an establishment with unrenewed permits or iqamas, or a worker three months in the Kingdom without a permit; item 4 bars transfers to an employer with collective wage delay, tasattur, or workers left to work for others.  |  This is the 2021 "labour mobility" reform as now codified in the Regulation: an expatriate can leave at contract expiry, or after twelve months in the Kingdom on ninety days' notice, and Qiwa processes the transfer without the employer's release. The employer's protection is Art. 77 (compensation for early termination of a fixed-term contract, preserved by item 21) and the Qiwa contract term. The three-months-unpaid-wages ground links directly to WPS data on Mudad. Item 2 and item 4 mean a receiving employer with lapsed permits or WPS arrears cannot take anyone in, whatever the worker's rights.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 Second items 19, 20 (corporate changes are not transfers)  |  The following are name amendments, not transfers of services: continuation of a deceased owner's business by heirs (with power of attorney or conversion to a company, Ministry of Commerce approval); change of the establishment's name, objects, all or some partners, or company type with the commercial register retained (MoC approval); merger of two or more establishments whether or not the legal entity changes, stated in the constitutional document (MoC approval); separation of a partner with a share of activities as a sole business (MoC approval); conversion of a sole establishment into a company by admission of partners; transfer of ownership of the establishment with all workers continuing and moving to the new owner with the register retained (MoC approval). Moving non-Saudi workforce registration between branches of the same entity (without limit) or between entities of the same establishment is a workforce-registration transfer, not a transfer of services, under Nitaqat.  |  For M&A: a share deal, a merger, or a business transfer with the commercial register retained does not require individual Qiwa transfers or the workers' consent under item 14, and does not consume the buyer's Nitaqat headroom; an asset deal into a new commercial register does, worker by worker, with each expatriate's consent and the receiving entity's Nitaqat band as gates. Structure the deal with this table in view and put the Qiwa "name amendment" step on the closing checklist.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Labor Law Art. 90 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H)  |  Wages and all sums due to the worker must be paid in the official currency: daily-paid workers at least weekly, monthly-paid workers monthly, piece-work with weekly instalments proportionate to work done and the balance within the week after delivery, others at least weekly. Establishments are obliged to pay wages into the workers' accounts through banks approved in the Kingdom, provided the due dates above are not exceeded; the Minister may exempt some establishments. (The original text, which made bank payment optional with the worker's consent and required payment at the workplace during working hours, is no longer in force.)  |  Bank payment of wages is a statutory duty since 2015, not a WPS convenience: cash wages, or wages paid after the monthly due date, breach Art. 90 regardless of the Wage Protection System, and are penalised under Art. 229 through the Minister's schedule (the original Art. 235 fine of SAR 500-3,000 per worker is no longer in force). The WPS adds the monthly reporting and matching layer (next two rows).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Social Insurance Law Arts. 7, 8(4), 9, 10 (GOSI online)  |  The employer must register its establishment and each contributor with GOSI within the Regulation's grace period, submit wage data in the manner GOSI sets, and pay both shares of contributions within the Regulation's period (2% per month delay fine); government bodies and state-controlled companies must demand a GOSI certificate before paying dues, awarding tenders, amending or renewing the commercial register, licensing, or approving recruitment from abroad. See `social-insurance-law.md`.  |  Registration, monthly wage updates and payment run through the GOSI online portal, which is integrated with Qiwa (a new Qiwa contract is expected to match a GOSI registration at the same wage) and with the Ministry of Commerce (commercial-register renewal checks GOSI compliance) `[model knowledge — verify]` for the integrations. The statutory duty and the certificate rule are settled.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Exit/re-entry and final-exit visas (Absher / Muqeem; Labor Law Art. 40)  |  The Labor Law fixes only that the employer bears exit and re-entry fees and the return ticket at the end of the relationship (Art. 40). The visas themselves are Ministry of Interior residency acts issued on Absher (business) or Muqeem: single or multiple exit/re-entry during employment, and a final-exit visa (خروج نهائي) when the relationship ends, after which the iqama is cancelled. Since the 2021 labour-mobility reform an expatriate can request exit/re-entry and final exit himself through Absher with notice to the employer, removing the employer's veto.  |  Termination-review: the final-exit visa is the operational end of an expatriate's employment and is normally issued after the end-of-service settlement; issuing it before settlement, or withholding it after settlement, are both common dispute triggers. Only the fee-allocation in Art. 40 is settled; the Absher/Muqeem mechanics and the worker-initiated exit rule are practice points to verify.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 40 fees; `[model knowledge — verify]` for the Absher/Muqeem mechanics and worker-initiated exit 

FILE: social-insurance-law
 Art. 7  |  The Regulation sets: how employers and contributors register; a grace period for employers to register contributors who meet the conditions and to de-register those who no longer do; a grace period for a contributor to register or de-register himself if the employer fails or delays; and the rules for registration after the grace periods lapse.  |  The registration deadline is in the Regulation, not the law; the GOSI practice deadline is 15 days from joining and 15 days from leaving, applied through the GOSI online portal `[model knowledge — verify]`. Under Art. 31(2) an injury before registration and after the grace period is uncompensated by GOSI, which pushes the cost onto the employer.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15 with Royal Decree M/273 clause Third(1)-(3)  |  Annuities contribution: 22% of the contributory wage, 11% employer and 11% contributor (optional contributors bear the whole 22%). The Decree phases the rate in for those covered by the new law: 18% during the 12 months from the effective date; 19% from the month following the lapse of 12 months; 20% from the month following 24 months; 21% from the month following 36 months; 22% from the month following 48 months; the employer bears 50% and the contributor 50% of the compulsory contribution at each step. The Council of Ministers may amend the rates on the Board's proposal after an actuarial study.  |  For employees who joined the scheme on or after 3 July 2024: 9% + 9% (from 3 July 2024), 9.5% + 9.5% (from mid-2025), 10% + 10% (from mid-2026), 10.5% + 10.5% (from mid-2027), 11% + 11% (from mid-2028). The exact switch-over month is ambiguous in the text: "the month following the lapse of twelve months from the effective date" reads as August 2025 on a strict count from 3 July 2024, while GOSI has applied each step from July `[model knowledge — verify]`. Employees with pre-July 2024 periods stay on the 1421H rate of 9% + 9% (row on legacy Art. 18); the Decree's phase-in applies only to "those covered by the new law". A hiring-review cost model therefore needs the hire date and prior-GOSI status of each Saudi employee, not just the salary.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 16 with Council of Ministers Decision 1022 clause Third(5)  |  The statutory entitlement age is 65. A contributor is entitled to a pension on reaching 65 with at least the minimum contribution period set by Council of Ministers decision; Decision 1022 sets that minimum at 180 months. Early pension: up to 120 months before 65 if the contributor has stopped work subject to the law and has at least 360 months of contributions (subject to the Art. 17(3) reduction).  |  Retirement age for new entrants is 65 (both sexes), against 60 under the 1421H law. Labor Law Art. 74(4), as amended by Royal Decree M/134 of 27/11/1440H, now ends the contract on "the worker reaching retirement age as provided in the Social Insurance Law, unless the parties agree to continue" (the original 60 men / 55 women wording is no longer in force), so the Labor Law retirement trigger follows this row: 65 for new-law contributors, 60 for legacy contributors, or the Decree clause Fifth phased age in between. Termination-review must first classify the employee (new-law, legacy, or phased) before treating "retirement age" as a lawful end of contract.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 17, 26  |  Pension = 2.25% × average contributory wage (average of the highest 180 months of contributory wages, Art. 26) × months of contribution ÷ 12, capped at 100% of that average; the Board may add benefits above the cap. Minimum pension SAR 4,000 per month at 480 months, reduced proportionately for each 12 months (and each month) short of 480, with a floor of SAR 2,000 at the 180-month qualifying period. Early pension is reduced by 3% for each 12 months (pro rata per month) before 65 or short of 480 months, whichever is less, for the life of the pension; a pension claimed after continuing work beyond 65 is increased by 3% per 12 months.  |  Compared with the 1421H law (2.5% accrual on the last 24 months' average, Art. 38 legacy), the new formula accrues more slowly and on a longer averaging window. Relevant to termination-review only as context for early-retirement negotiations; the numbers are GOSI's to compute, and a skill must not compute an individual's pension from this row.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 25, 27  |  A contributor who reaches 65 (or is disabled under Art. 21) and stops covered work without qualifying for a pension receives: with 60 months or more of contributions, a compensation based on the total employer and contributor contributions revalued (not less than the total paid), payable monthly or as a lump sum at his option; with less than 60 months, a lump sum on the same basis. On death without pension entitlement the family receives the full lump sum. A contributor who loses Saudi nationality receives a lump sum.  |  The employer's contributions are not refundable to the employer in any scenario; they follow the contributor. No employer-side action is needed at termination beyond de-registration (Art. 7).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 28, 29 with Decree clause Third(4)  |  The occupational hazards and additional compensations branch applies compulsorily to all employees and all workers working inside the Kingdom (no nationality condition). Contribution: 2% of the contributory wage, borne by the employer; the Board may, for a fixed period, raise it up to double for employers who refuse to comply with instructions on contributors' safety and health; the Council of Ministers may amend the rate after an actuarial study. The date on which the branch applies to public employees is set by Council of Ministers decision.  |  Every non-Saudi employee costs the employer 2% of contributory wage (capped at SAR 45,000 base) and nothing else at GOSI. Every Saudi employee costs 2% plus the annuities and unemployment shares. The doubling power is the only GOSI-side sanction tied to OSH non-compliance; the main one is Labor Law Art. 229 as amended by Royal Decree M/46 of 1436H (fine up to SAR 100,000, closure up to 30 days or permanently, per-person multiplication).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 41, 42 with Decree clause Sixth(1)  |  Maternity compensation: a female contributor who gives birth receives compensation for 3 months (plus one month where the child is sick or disabled and needs constant care), if she has at least 12 months of contributions, continuous or not, in the 36 months before the birth and is in covered employment at the birth, and the birth is at least six months into the pregnancy. The compensation is 100% of the average contributory wage of the 12 months before the birth, payable from the first day of the birth month (claimable one month before the expected date), and may not be combined with wages. The Decree extends maternity compensation to contributors under the 1421H law's occupational-hazards branch, counting the qualifying period from 3 July 2024.  |  GOSI now pays three months of maternity compensation for qualifying Saudi employees (and, through the Decree, for 1421H-law contributors in the occupational-hazards branch, which includes non-Saudi women). How this interacts with the employer's paid maternity leave under Labor Law Art. 151 (12 weeks fully paid after the 1446H amendment `[model knowledge — verify]`) is a live question: the law bars combining the compensation with wages, which suggests the employer's wage obligation is displaced for the covered months, but GOSI's implementing rules were not available. Do not advise on the split without the Regulation.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 45-49  |  SANED benefit conditions: Saudi nationality; qualifying period met; not dismissed for a reason attributable to the worker; no private business or work income; did not leave voluntarily; able to work; below pension age; registered within the Regulation's deadline after leaving; actively seeking work; complying with training. Qualifying periods: 12 months in the 36 before the first claim, 18 for the second, 24 for the third, 36 in 48 thereafter. Benefit: up to 12 months per entitlement within 24 months; 60% of the average monthly wage (1/24 of the last 24 months' contributory wages) for the first three months and 50% thereafter, capped at SAR 9,000 then SAR 7,500 per month. Payment stops on death, pension entitlement, or loss of any condition.  |  Termination-review point: the reason code the employer records when de-registering the employee at GOSI (resignation, Art. 77 termination, Art. 80 dismissal) determines SANED eligibility; a dismissal recorded under Labor Law Art. 80 disqualifies the worker, so employees contest the coding `[model knowledge — verify]`. Mutual-separation agreements should state the exit category and who submits the GOSI notification.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 54, 57  |  Claims for daily injury allowances and travel/accommodation allowances lapse one year after they fall due; claims for other benefits lapse after five years, unless GOSI accepts an excuse. Cash benefits may be attached only by court judgment, up to 25% for one creditor and 50% for several, maintenance debts ranking first; GOSI itself has a priority right to set off its own claims up to 25% (more where the benefit is a lump sum or the debt arose from incorrect data).  |  An employer creditor (loan, training bond) cannot reach GOSI benefits except through a judgment and within the 25% cap.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Royal Decree M/273 clauses Second, Fourth, Fifth  |  The new law does not apply to (1) contributors with contribution periods before 3 July 2024 under the Civil Retirement Law or the 1421H law that have not been compensated, or (2) pensioners under those laws; for them the 1421H law, the Civil Retirement Law and the Unemployment Insurance Law of 1435H remain in force. By exception, contributors with fewer than 240 months of such periods who were under 50 Hijri years (48 years 6 months Gregorian) on 3 July 2024 get a phased pension age: under 29 → 65; 29 → 64y 8m; 30 → 64y 4m; 31 → 64; 32 → 63y 8m; 33 → 63y 4m; 34 → 63; 35 → 62y 8m; 36 → 62y 4m; 37 → 62; 38 → 61y 8m; 39 → 61y 4m; 40 → 61; 41 → 60y 8m; 42 → 60y 4m; 43 → 60; 44 → 59y 8m; 45 → 59y 4m; 46 → 59; 47 → 58y 8m; 48 to under 48y 6m → 58y 4m (age bands are "X years or more and under X+1"). Early-pension qualifying period for that group: fewer than 180 months at 3 July 2024 → 360 months; 180-191 → 348; 192-203 → 336; 204-215 → 324; 216-227 → 312; 228-239 → 300.  |  The "who is on which law" test is: any GOSI or civil-retirement contribution month before 3 July 2024 that was not paid out as a lump sum keeps the employee on the 1421H law (rates, formula, branches), but with the new retirement age from this table if the employee was under 48.5 and had under 240 months on that date. An employee aged 48.5 or more, or with 240+ months, keeps age 60. A workforce census by hire date, age on 3 July 2024 and prior contribution months is the only way to cost retirement timing.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 1421H law Art. 4, 5, 7 (legacy contributors)  |  Occupational-hazards branch applies compulsorily to all workers without distinction of sex, nationality or age; annuities branch compulsorily to all Saudi workers under 60 when first covered (special rules for returners aged 60-65). Condition: work under a contract of employment for one or more employers, whatever the contract's term, nature, form or wage, performed mainly inside the Kingdom, or a Saudi working abroad for a Kingdom-headquartered employer. Excluded: civil servants and military under their own schemes, foreign staff of foreign missions, agricultural and herding workers, seafarers, domestic servants, foreign workers on jobs normally under three months, home-workers, family members in family-only businesses (with opt-in for the last two). Employers must apply the law from the day the conditions are met; the Regulation sets registration.  |  For pre-July 2024 hires the exclusion list is closed (domestic workers, sub-three-month foreign assignments, etc.). For a short-term foreign assignee under the legacy regime the three-month "normally" test applies; under the new law the test is a visa of 60 days or less subject to a Board decision (Art. 6). Because new hires are on the new law, the legacy exclusions matter only for the existing population.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 1421H law Art. 20, 38 (as amended by M/49 of 1431H and M/134 of 1440H), 62 (as replaced by M/55 of 1438H and M/81 of 1445H) (legacy contributors)  |  Contributions and delay fines are secured by a lien ranking after wages; GOSI may attach the employer's assets after a court order once schedules are certified by the Minister. Retirement pension at 60 with at least 120 months of contributions; early pension before 60 with 300 months for a contributor no longer covered; sentence of imprisonment with 120 months lets the family draw it; a contributor at 60 with at least five years may buy up to five notional years (para 2 as reworded by M/49). The portal records that M/49 of 1431H reworded para 1(c) (a female contributor no longer covered may draw her pension at 55 with 120 months) and that M/134 of 1440H then deleted a paragraph of the article; read with M/134's simultaneous amendment of Labor Law Art. 74(4), the deleted paragraph is the women-at-55 provision, so the legacy age is 60 for both sexes `[model knowledge — verify]` for the identification of the deleted paragraph, which the portal note does not name. Pension formula: 1/40 of the average wage of the last 24 months per year of contribution, capped at 100% of that average, the average not to exceed 150% of the wage at the start of the last five years. Penalties (Art. 62 in force): a fine up to SAR 10,000 for any violation of the law or regulations, including submitting incorrect data or withholding data GOSI requests, doubled on repetition, multiplied by the number of contributors concerned and by the number of data items submitted or withheld; registering a person who does not work for the employer: that fine or up to twice the contributions for the period, whichever is higher, per person; wrongful benefits recovered plus a fine up to their amount; office committees investigate and refer to the Governor, who issues the decision, with objection within 30 days under Art. 61; publication of the decision may be ordered; no fine after five years; GOSI may settle and waive fines (para 8, added by M/81 of 19/5/1445H).  |  The original Art. 62 (SAR 5,000 per violation with a SAR 50,000 aggregate cap) is not in force; since M/55 of 1438H the legacy ceiling is SAR 10,000 per violation with per-contributor and per-data-item multipliers and no aggregate cap, and the ghost-registration offence and the five-year limitation already existed under the legacy law. The new law's Art. 59 raises the per-violation ceiling to SAR 50,000. Which regime applies to a violation concerning a legacy contributor after 3 July 2024 is not settled by the text (Decree clause Fourth keeps the 1421H "provisions" in force for those contributors); assume the new law's Art. 59 for anything GOSI enforces today and flag the point `[model knowledge — verify]`.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 

FILE: saudization-nitaqat
 Labor Law Art. 26  |  All establishments, whatever their activity or headcount, must work to attract and employ Saudis, provide the means for them to stay in work, and give them the opportunity to prove their fitness through guidance, training and qualification. Saudi workers must be not less than 75% of the employer's total workforce; the Minister may temporarily reduce the percentage where technical competencies or academic qualifications are unavailable or posts cannot be filled by nationals.  |  The 75% is the statutory default; every Nitaqat threshold below 75% is an exercise of the Minister's reduction power, which is why HRSD can raise quotas by decision without amending the law. A contract or diligence memo should cite Art. 26(2) as the source of the duty and Nitaqat as the current measure of it.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Implementing Regulation Art. 8 (to Art. 26)  |  In implementation of Art. 26(2), the Saudization ratios establishments must achieve are set through programs the ministry designs, which track the ratio for each private-sector activity and withhold all or some ministry services from establishments that do not achieve the set ratio, "such as the Program for Incentivising Establishments to Localise Jobs (Nitaqat)"; the ministry updates these programs periodically whenever needed.  |  This is the regulatory anchor for Nitaqat and for the service-withholding sanction; it also signals that the program's parameters are administrative and change without notice in the law. Any Nitaqat percentage quoted in a memo must carry a date and the phase it belongs to.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Nitaqat guide, Services by Range  |  Platinum, High Green and Medium Green: may apply for new expatriate visas, change expatriate workers' occupations, renew work permits, transfer expatriates' sponsorship into the entity from any other entity, and benefit from immediate counting in Nitaqat. Low Green: cannot apply for new visas or change occupations; can renew existing work permits; immediate counting. Red: cannot change occupations, cannot transfer expatriates in, cannot apply for new visas, cannot issue work permits for new expatriates, cannot renew work permits of existing expatriates. Use of each service is subject to its own regulations.  |  Red is existential for an expatriate-heavy business: existing work permits lapse at renewal, which makes continued employment of the expatriate unlawful (Labor Law Art. 33) and exposes the employer to per-person penalties under Art. 229 as amended (fine up to SAR 100,000, closure) and the worker to removal through the Ministry of Interior referral in Art. 39 as amended. Low Green freezes growth (no new visas, no occupation change) but keeps the current workforce. "Immediate counting" means a Green-or-better entity gets credit for a new Saudi hire at once, whereas a Red entity is counted on the rolling average only `[model knowledge — verify]` for the mechanics beyond the guide's one-line list.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Labor Law Arts. 32-34; Art. 35 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H and again by M/44 of 1446H)  |  Recruitment from abroad requires ministry approval. A non-Saudi may not work, and may not be allowed to work, without a work permit from the ministry, granted only if the worker entered lawfully and is authorised to work, has qualifications or skills the country needs and that nationals lack or lack in sufficient numbers (or is ordinary labour the country needs), and is contracted with and under the responsibility of an employer. Other licences do not substitute for the work permit. Art. 35 in force: the ministry may, for considerations it assesses, refuse to renew a work permit where the employer has breached the job-localisation standards the ministry sets or any other requirements or controls in the Regulation; the Regulation sets procedures ensuring the worker is not harmed by the non-renewal, including the possibility of transferring the worker's services to another employer without the violating employer's consent. (The original Art. 35, requiring a check that no qualified Saudi job-seeker wants the job before renewal, is no longer in force.)  |  Art. 35 as amended is the direct statutory basis for the Nitaqat service block on renewals: a Red entity is denied the Art. 33 permit for new hires and the Art. 35 renewal for existing ones, and the worker may then transfer out without the employer's consent (Regulation Art. 14 Second item 6, see `platform-obligations.md`). The employer therefore loses the workforce, not only the growth headroom, when it falls to Red.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Qiwa contract documentation as a condition of Nitaqat credit (from 15 April 2026, as claimed)  |  From 15 April 2026 a Saudi employee is counted in the Nitaqat Saudization rate only if his employment contract is documented (authenticated) on Qiwa; GOSI registration alone no longer earns credit.  |  If confirmed, an entity's Nitaqat rate can fall without any change in headcount simply because contracts were never authenticated on Qiwa. Diligence should reconcile GOSI-registered Saudis with Qiwa-documented contracts. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.5 (eiglaw.com note), unconfirmed on hrsd.gov.sa or qiwa.sa. See `platform-obligations.md` for the underlying documentation duty (Regulation Art. 18(2)), which is settled.  |  `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Nitaqat 2026-2028 phase (from 26 April 2026, as claimed)  |  A new Nitaqat phase for 2026-2028 with higher required Saudization rates by sector, announced by HRSD with a lead time before each annual step; the lead also claims the Yellow band was removed (inconsistent with the 2021 guide, which already had no Yellow band). Entities that were Green under the 2024-2025 coefficients may fall a band on 26 April 2026 without a workforce change, as happened in the guide's own 2023-to-2024 example.  |  Any current-band statement for a target must rest on the Qiwa dashboard after 26 April 2026, not on the 2024 coefficients in this file. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.5 (middleeastbriefing.com), unconfirmed.  |  `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Revised work-visa quotas for new establishments (26 August 2026, as claimed)  |  Instant-visa caps for newly established businesses tied to company age, participation in the Establishment Programme, and Nitaqat band.  |  Relevant to hiring-review for a newly incorporated Saudi entity that expects to recruit expatriates in its first year. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.2 (visasupdate.com, low reliability), unconfirmed.  |  `[model knowledge — verify]` 

FILE: occupational-safety
 Law Art. 122  |  The employer must take the precautions necessary to protect workers from hazards and diseases arising from the work and the machinery used, and to protect and secure the work; must post in a visible place the instructions on work and worker safety in Arabic and, where needed, in any other language the workers understand; and may not charge workers or deduct from their wages any amount for providing this protection.  |  Posting is a condition of the Art. 80(2) summary-dismissal ground (breach of "posted" safety instructions); termination-review should ask where and in which languages the instructions were posted. Any PPE charge-back or deduction is void.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Law Art. 137 (M/46 of 1436H)  |  Temporary disability from a work injury: financial aid equal to the full wage for sixty days, then 75% of the wage for the whole treatment period; if treatment reaches one year, or it is medically determined that recovery is unlikely and the worker cannot work, the injury is deemed total disability, the contract is terminated and the worker is compensated for the injury; the employer may not recover what was paid during that year.  |  The original text of the [معدلة] article in `boe/labor-law-ar.md` and the BOE English main text print thirty days; the sixty-day figure is the M/46 amending block (and the English appendix). Termination on total disability is a lawful ground distinct from Art. 82 (no termination for ordinary sickness until sick leave is exhausted). termination-review must not treat a work-injury absence as sick leave under Art. 117.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Law Art. 139  |  The employer is not bound by Arts. 133, 137 and 138 if it is established that the worker deliberately injured himself, the injury resulted from the worker's intentional misconduct, or the worker refused without legitimate reason to see a physician or to accept treatment by the physician the employer designated.  |  The burden is on the employer ("إذا ثبت"); an internal-investigation file on the incident is the evidence. Negligence short of intentional misconduct does not disqualify.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Law Art. 143 and Reg. Art. 5(6)  |  The employer must have one or more physicians examine, comprehensively and at least once a year, workers exposed to any occupational disease in the GOSI schedules, and record the results in its records and the workers' files; the Regulations require a medical-examination register in establishments where occupational disease is possible, listing examined workers, dates and results.  |  Annual examinations and the register are what a termination-review checks before accepting "unfit for work" as a ground; the 1447H occupational-fitness examination regulation (Decision 33232 of 11/3/1447H) is listed on the HRSD page but was not read `[model knowledge — verify]`.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` (Reg. Art. 5: `[authority — HRSD]`) 
 Law Art. 80(2) (M/46) and Art. 81(6)  |  Summary dismissal without award, notice or compensation is available where the worker, despite a written warning, deliberately disregards the safety instructions for work and workers that the employer posted in a visible place (Art. 80(2)); the worker may leave without notice keeping all statutory rights where the workplace has a serious hazard threatening his safety or health of which the employer knew and took no steps indicating its removal (Art. 81(6)).  |  Both grounds require documentary proof (posting, written warning, knowledge of the hazard). Art. 80 as amended read from the M/46 amending block in `boe/labor-law-ar.md`; Art. 81 carries no [معدلة] marker. Art. 82 (no termination for illness before sick leave is exhausted) applies to ordinary illness; work-injury absence is governed by Art. 137.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Law Art. 229 (M/46 of 1436H), replacing old Art. 236  |  Any breach of the Law, Regulations or implementing decisions is punishable by a fine up to SAR 100,000, closure up to 30 days, or permanent closure, singly or combined; doubled on repetition; fines multiplied by the number of persons concerned. The original Art. 236 (fine SAR 3,000–10,000 per violation and/or closure, with suspension of the hazard source, for breach of Part VIII Chapters 1–2 and the Art. 121 rules) was consolidated into this article.  |  The specific safety-violation fines sit in the ministerial schedule (not provided); closure is in principle available for safety breaches. Read from the M/46 amending block in `boe/labor-law-ar.md`; the original text of Arts. 229–241 is the pre-M/46 penalty scheme.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 

FILE: playbook-defaults
| Governing law, acceptable | English law for international counterparties, with a Saudi-seated or offshore arbitration clause | Practice `[model knowledge — verify]`; enforcement of a foreign judgment or award runs through `enforcement-law.md` (from 2026-10-28) and the Enforcement Law 1433H until then |
| Liquidated damages and penalties | Accept a fixed compensation clause only with the statutory reduction in view: a Saudi court may reduce it where excessive or the obligation was partly performed, disallow it where no harm occurred, and the parties cannot contract out; pre-agreed compensation on a money debt is barred | `civil-transactions-law.md` Arts. 178-179 |
| Assignment | Assignment of a whole contract needs counterparty consent; advance consent valid on notice; a share sale does not transfer contracts | `civil-transactions-law.md` Arts. 98, 255 |
| Interest and late-payment charges | No contractual interest; pre-agreed compensation on money debts barred; state the position and flag any interest clause `[review]` | `civil-transactions-law.md` Arts. 178, 385; Sharia position `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Non-compete and non-solicit (commercial) | No statutory bar; reasonableness under general contract rules; employment non-competes are capped at two years and must be written | `civil-transactions-law.md` general rows; `labor-law.md` Art. 83 |
| Confidentiality survival | Confidentiality and dispute-resolution clauses survive termination by statute | `civil-transactions-law.md` Art. 113 |
| Board and shareholder formalities | Resolutions by circulation, notice periods, quorum and majorities from the Companies Law and the articles; listed companies add the CMA regulations | `companies-law.md`, `corporate-governance-regulations.md` |
## Labor (termination, hiring, classification, leave, policies)
| Contract types | Fixed-term (Arabic, documented on Qiwa) and indefinite; conversion and renewal rules per the Law | `labor-law.md` Arts. 51-58 |
| Probation | Up to 180 days total, in writing | `labor-law.md` Art. 53 |
| Notice on indefinite contracts | Employer 60 days, worker 30 days (monthly-paid) | `labor-law.md` Art. 75 |
| End-of-service award | Half a month's wage per year for the first five years, one month per year after, last wage as base, resignation fractions | `labor-law.md` Arts. 84-87 |
| Compensation for invalid termination | 15 days' wage per year of service (indefinite) or the remaining term (fixed), minimum two months, unless the contract fixes a figure | `labor-law.md` Art. 77 |
| Termination without award or notice | Only on the Art. 80 grounds after hearing the worker | `labor-law.md` Art. 80 |
| Non-compete | Written, maximum two years, limited to what protects a legitimate interest | `labor-law.md` Art. 83 |
| Working time | 8 hours a day or 48 a week; 6 and 36 in Ramadan for Muslims; overtime at hourly wage plus 50% | `labor-law.md` Arts. 98-108 |
| Leave | Annual 21 days rising to 30 after five years; sick leave 30 full, 60 three-quarters, 30 unpaid; maternity 12 weeks; paternity, marriage, bereavement, Hajj, exam leave per the Law | `labor-law.md` Arts. 109-117, 151-160 |
| Dispute route | Friendly settlement at HRSD, then the Labor Courts; 12-month limitation | `labor-dispute-route.md` |
| Work regulations | Employer's work regulations follow the HRSD model and need certification or approval | `labor-law-implementing-regulations.md` |

